/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * ib-sehs-deep.js — the plain-language teaching layer.
 *
 * ib-sehs-native.js holds the exam-register content: correct, dense, and
 * written at about a grade-13 reading level. This file adds the layer a
 * twelve-year-old needs on top of it, keyed by the same English section
 * title that ib-sehs-native.js uses:
 *
 *   metaphor  先这样想   an everyday picture FIRST, no jargon at all
 *   walk      详细讲解   the idea taken apart, one idea per paragraph
 *   numbers   算一算     the numbers actually worked through
 *   mistakes  易错点     the wrong ideas students arrive with
 *   why       为什么重要 where it shows up in sport
 *   quick     快速检查   a self-check, used only where the section has none
 *
 * Every field is optional and every section is independent, so this file
 * fills in progressively: a title that is absent simply renders exactly as
 * it did before. Bilingual pairs are {en, zh} and the renderer switches on
 * the page language like everything else.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
window.IB_DEEP = {

/* ─── Chapter 1 · Theme A ─ Inter- and intra-system communication ────── */

'Communication systems': {
metaphor:{en:'Your body has two ways to send a message, and they work nothing alike. The nervous system is a phone call: you tap someone on the shoulder and they move before you have finished the sentence. The endocrine system is a noticeboard: you write a note, pin it up, and everyone who reads it over the next hour acts on it. A phone call and a billboard — same information, completely different timing.',
zh:'身体有两种传递信息的方式，它们的工作方式完全不同。神经系统像一通电话：你轻拍某人的肩膀，他还没等你说完话就已经动了。内分泌系统像一块公告板：你写一张便条钉上去，接下来一小时内看到的人都会照做。同样是传递信息，时间方式却天差地别。'},
walk:[
{en:'A nerve is an electrical wire with a chemical gap in the middle. The message races along the wire as a spike of electricity. But wires do not touch each other — there is always a tiny space between one nerve ending and the next cell. That space is called a synapse. To cross it, the first cell squirts out a chemical called a neurotransmitter, and the chemical drifts across and knocks on a receptor on the far side. So a nerve signal is electricity, then a chemical, then electricity again. Think of a relay race, where each runner hands the baton to the next one instead of running the whole track themselves.',
zh:'神经是一根电线，中间却隔着一道化学的缝。信息以电流脉冲的形式沿着电线飞速前进。但两根线并不直接相接——一个神经末梢和下一个细胞之间总有极小的空隙，这个空隙叫突触。要越过它，第一个细胞会释放一种叫神经递质的化学物质，这种物质漂过去，敲击另一侧的受体。于是神经信号是：电、化学、再变成电。像一场接力赛，每个跑者把接力棒交给下一位，而不是自己跑完整段。'},
{en:'Hormones travel a completely different route. A gland is a small organ whose job is to make chemicals, and it releases hormones straight into the bloodstream. The blood then carries them everywhere in the body — which sounds powerful, and it is. But here is the part students miss: the hormone does not automatically do its job to every cell it touches. It only works on a target cell, and a cell only counts as a target if it carries the right receptor, shaped like a lock that fits that particular hormone. Every other cell lets the hormone float straight past. That is why hormones can have such wide effects and such precise ones at the same time — they visit every cell in the body, and only some of them have the matching lock.',
zh:'激素走的是完全不同的路线。腺体是一个专门制造化学物质的小器官，它把激素直接释放到血液里。血液随后把它们带到全身——这听起来威力很大，确实如此。但这里是最容易漏掉的一点：激素并不会对每一个接触到的细胞都自动生效。它只对靶细胞起作用，而一个细胞要算靶细胞，必须带有相应的受体，受体的形状像一把恰好配得上这个激素的锁。其他所有细胞都会让激素径直飘过。���以激素既能产生很广泛的影响，又能很精确——它访问了全身每一个细胞，但只有一部分带着配对的锁。'},
{en:'Speed is the biggest difference between the two systems, and it is measured in completely different units. Nerve signals are timed in milliseconds. A knee reflex can be over in roughly fifty milliseconds — your leg has already pulled back before you consciously feel the tap. Hormones are timed in seconds, minutes, hours and days. Insulin can start pulling blood glucose down within minutes and keep working for hours afterwards. Growth hormone does its work over nights and weeks, mostly while you are asleep. So when a question asks you to compare the two systems, speed and reach are the two things that earn the mark.',
zh:'两个系统最大的差别是速度，而它们用的时间单位完全不同。神经信号以毫秒计。膝反射大约五十毫秒就完成了——你的腿已经缩回来，你才刚意识到被敲了一下。激素以秒、分、小时和天计。胰岛素可以在几分钟内开始降低血糖，并且在之后数小时里持续发挥作用。生长激素则在你睡着的那些夜晚和几周里慢慢起作用。所以当题目要你比较这两个系统时，能得分的正是速度和范围这两点。'},
{en:'Reach is the other big difference. Nerves are local and precise. One nerve carries messages to one small group of muscles in one forearm, and nothing else. A hormone is the opposite — it circulates everywhere, so one hormone can reach bone, skin, liver and muscle at the same time. This is also the flip side of a problem: a side effect you did not want can be felt all over the body, because there is no way to aim a hormone at just one tissue.',
zh:'范围是另一个大差别。神经是局部而精确的。一根神经只把信息送到前臂某一小群肌肉，不会送给别处。激素正好相反——它随血液循环到全身，所以一种激素可以同时到达骨、皮肤、肝脏和肌肉。这同时也是问题的另一面：你不想要的副作用会全身都能感觉到，因为没有办法把激素只瞄准某一种组织。'}],
numbers:{en:'Line the timings up and the pattern is easy to remember. A reflex arc — stimulus to muscle response — runs about 30 to 50 milliseconds. A hormonal response: adrenaline begins lifting heart rate in roughly 30 seconds to a couple of minutes; insulin works on a scale of minutes to hours; growth hormone and testosterone shape adaptation over weeks to months. A habit worth building for the exam: whenever a question asks you to explain a control mechanism, ask two questions of it — how fast, and how far. Fast and local means neural. Slow and wide means endocrine. That one habit answers most of the comparison questions.',
zh:'把时间线排在一起，规律就很容易记住。反射弧——从刺激到肌肉反应——大约需要30到50毫秒。内分泌反应：肾上腺素大约在30秒到两三分钟内开始提高心率；胰岛素以分钟到小时为尺度；生长激素和睾酮则以数周到数月来塑造适应。备考时值得养成一个习惯：每当题目要你解释一个调控机制，先问它两个问题——多快？多远？又快又局部就是神经；又慢又广泛就是内分泌。光是这个习惯，就能回答大部分比较类的题目。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"The nervous system is electrical and the endocrine system is chemical." This is only half right and it is a very common loss of a mark. Nerves are electrical along the wire but chemical across every synapse. Endocrine signalling is chemical for the whole journey. Saying "nerves are electrical" with no qualification is the kind of answer that sounds confident and scores partial.',
zh:'“神经系统是电的，内分泌系统是化学的。”这只对了一半，也是非常常见的丢分点。神经在电线部分是电的，但每跨过一个突触就是化学的。内分泌信号全程都是化学的。只说“神经是电的”而不加限定，听起来很有把握，其实只能拿到部分分。'},
{en:'"Hormones act everywhere." They travel everywhere, but they only act on target cells carrying the right receptor. Everywhere else is a passenger, not a destination.',
zh:'“激素作用于全身各处。”它们的确会到达全身，但只对带有相应受体的靶细胞起作用。其他地方只是乘客，不是目的地。'},
{en:'"Adrenaline works instantly because it is fast in sport." It travels fast because the blood moves fast, not because it is a nerve signal. It is still measurably slower than a nerve impulse, and an answer that treats it as instant confuses the two systems.',
zh:'“肾上腺素在运动中起效很快，所以是瞬间的。”它传得快是因为血流快，不是因为它是神经信号。它仍然可以测出地比神经冲动慢，把它当成瞬时的答案混淆了两个系统。'}],
why:{en:'A sprint is the cleanest example of both systems running at once. The gun fires. Your nervous system has already asked your leg muscles to contract in well under a second, because the alternative — waiting for adrenaline to arrive — would leave you standing on the blocks. Adrenaline then takes over for the next few minutes, raising heart rate, redirecting blood to the muscles and freeing glucose. Sport does not choose between the two systems. It uses the fast one to start and the slow one to keep going.',
zh:'短跑是两个系统同时工作的最干净的例子。枪响的那一刻，你的神经系统已经在不到一秒内让腿部肌肉收缩了，因为另一种选择——等肾上腺素送过来——会让你一直站在起跑器上。接着肾上腺素接手接下来的几分钟，提高心率，把血液重新分配到肌肉，并释放葡萄糖。运动并不是在两个系统之间二选一，而是用快的那个起步，用慢的那个续航。'}},

'Neural pathways and coordination': {
metaphor:{en:'Think of your body as a two-storey building with a control room on the top floor. The brain and spinal cord are the control room: they receive every report, decide what to do, and send the orders down. Everything else — all the wires running through the building — is the peripheral nervous system. The wires do not decide anything. They carry. A message can go up to the control room, or come down from it, but the decision is always made upstairs.',
zh:'把身体想成一栋两层楼，顶层是控制室。脑和脊髓就是控制室：它们接收所有报告、决定要做什么、再把命令发下去。楼里其余的一切——所有穿行的线路——就是周围神经系统。线路不做任何决定，它们只负责运送。信息可以送进控制室，也可以从控制室发下来，但决定永远是在楼上做出的。'},
walk:[
{en:'The central nervous system — the brain and the spinal cord — is the processing centre. The spinal cord is especially worth understanding on its own terms: it is not just a cable running between the brain and the body. It is a decision-making centre in its own right. That is why a knee jerk still works when the spinal cord is intact but the brain is not being consulted. The cord can handle a simple, fast, protective job entirely on its own.',
zh:'中枢神经系统——脑和脊髓——是处理中心。脊髓尤其值得单独理解：它不只是一条连接脑和身体 的缆线，它本身就是决策中心。这正是膝反射在脊髓完好、而大脑并未参与时仍然能工作的原因。脊髓完全可以独自完成这种简单、快速、保护性的任务。'},
{en:'The peripheral nervous system is everything outside the brain and spinal cord, and it comes in two functionally different halves. The somatic system drives skeletal muscle — the muscle you can consciously choose to move, like the muscles of your arm when you decide to jump. The autonomic system runs everything you cannot consciously control: glands, smooth muscle in your organs and gut, and the cardiac muscle of your heart. You can decide to raise your arm. You cannot decide to raise your heart rate, and you would not want to be able to.',
zh:'周围神经系统是脑和脊髓之外的一切，它在功能上分成两半。躯体系统驱动骨骼肌——你可以有意识决定让它运动的肌肉，比如你决定起跳时手臂的肌肉。自主系统负责所有你无法有意识控制的东西：腺体、器官和肠道里的平滑肌，以及心脏的心肌。你可以决定抬起手臂，但你无法决定提高心率，而且你也不会希望自己能决定。'},
{en:'The autonomic system then splits again in two, and the two halves pull in opposite directions. The sympathetic division prepares you for action or stress. It raises heart rate, widens the airways, redirects blood to the muscles, and slows digestion. The parasympathetic division does the opposite: it supports recovery, digestion and saving energy. The textbook image is that these are two ends of one accelerator and one brake. A useful correction to that image: they are not simply on and off. Most of the time both are active, and what changes is the balance between them.',
zh:'自主系统又分成两半，两半的方向相反。交感分支为行动或压力做准备：提高心率、扩大气道、把血液重新分配到肌肉、减慢消化。副交感分支正相反：支持恢复、消化和能量节省。教科书的比喻是，这就像同一辆车的一个油门和一个刹车。一个有用的补充是：它们并不是简单的开和关。多数时候两者都在工作，变化的是它们之间的平衡。'},
{en:'After exercise the balance shifts. Parasympathetic activity rises, heart rate falls back toward resting levels, and digestion and repair can proceed. At the same time the endocrine system is doing slower, longer work: growth hormone and insulin support tissue repair, energy regulation and longer-term adaptation. So recovery is not one switch. It is the nervous system winding down while the hormonal system quietly rebuilds.',
zh:'运动结束后，平衡会移动。副交感活动上升，心率回落到接近静息水平，消化和修复得以进行。与此同时，内分泌系统在做更慢、更持久的工作：生长激素和胰岛素支持组织修复、能量调节和长期适应。所以恢复并不是一个开关，而是神经系统慢慢降下来，同时激素系统悄悄地重建。'}],
numbers:{en:'Here is a way to make the two halves of the autonomic system stick. At rest, take a resting heart rate of about 60 beats per minute. Sympathetic activation can push it to 180 or more in a maximal effort; the parasympathetic system is what brings it back down afterwards, typically by around 10 to 20 beats per minute once recovery starts. The exam habit: whenever a question gives you a change in heart rate, ask which division is being described and in which direction it is pushing.',
zh:'这里有一个能让自主系统两半都记住的办法。静息心率大约是每分钟60次。交感激活可以在最大努力时把它推到180次甚至更高；副交感系统负责之后把它降下来，恢复开始后通常以每分钟10到20次的幅度回落。备考习惯：每当题目给你一个心率变化，先问这是在说哪一分支，以及它往哪个方向推。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"The spinal cord only passes messages to the brain." The single most important correction here. The cord is a processing centre in its own right, which is exactly why reflexes are integrated there and can happen without the brain.',
zh:'“脊髓只是把信息传给大脑。”这是这一节最重要的纠正。脊髓本身就是处理中心，这正是反射在脊髓中整合、并且不需要大脑参与就能发生的原因。'},
{en:'"Autonomic means involuntary, so the heart is not muscle." Cardiac muscle is muscle, and it is under autonomic control. Involuntary describes who is in charge, not what the tissue is made of.',
zh:'“自主就是不受意志控制，所以心脏不是肌肉。”心肌就是肌肉，而且受自主神经支配。不受意志控制说的是“谁在指挥”，不是“这种组织由什么构成”。'},
{en:'"Sympathetic and parasympathetic are simply on and off." Both are usually active at once; what changes is the balance. Treat them as a dimmer switch, not a light switch.',
zh:'“交感就是开，副交感就是关。”两者通常同时在工作，变化的是平衡。把它们当作调光开关，而不是普通开关。'}],
why:{en:'This is the wiring behind every moment of a performance. The sprinter who lifts their arms on the blocks is somatic control. The same athlete’s heart rate climbing from 60 to 190 in six seconds is autonomic sympathetic control, and the fact that they do not consciously control it is the point. Being able to name which system is doing the work, and how fast and how wide it acts, is what lets you explain a performance rather than just describe it.',
zh:'这就是每一次表现背后的线路。起跑器上撑起手臂的短跑运动员，用的是躯体控制；同一位运动员的心率在六秒内从60升到190，用的是自主交感控制，而恰恰是他们无法有意识控制这一点才说明了问题。能够指出是哪个系统在工作、它的速度和范围如何，才让你能够解释一次表现，而不只是描述它。'}},

'Systems working together': {
metaphor:{en:'Your organs are not separate machines sitting in the same room. They are one connected system, like the departments of a hospital sharing the same patient. The heart does not just pump; it depends on the lungs to fill it with oxygen and the blood to carry that oxygen to the muscles. The muscles do not just burn fuel; they need the gut to deliver it, the kidneys to manage waste, and the brain to tell them when to fire. Pull any one department out and the patient does not get slightly worse. They get much worse, all at once.',
zh:'你的器官不是各自独立、放在同一个房间里的机器。它们是一个连在一起的系统，就像同一家医院里各科室共享同一个病人。心脏不只是泵血，它依赖肺给它装满氧气，依赖血液把氧气送到肌肉。肌肉也不只是烧燃料，它们需要肠道把燃料送进来，需要肾脏处理废物，需要大脑告诉它们什么时候收缩。抽掉其中任何一个科室，病人不会只是稍微变差，而是会立刻、同时地大幅变差。'},
walk:[
{en:'Organ systems are interdependent. Nervous, endocrine, cardiovascular, respiratory, muscular and excretory systems all constrain one another, and they all maintain homeostasis together. A good way to picture it: homeostasis is not a single thermostat in one room. It is a building full of thermostats — temperature, blood glucose, blood pressure, pH, fluid balance, oxygen and carbon dioxide levels — all holding their own set points at the same time, all being nudged around constantly by exercise, by food and by the weather, and all being corrected by the same two systems you already know: nerves for fast local corrections, hormones for slow wide ones.',
zh:'各个器官系统是相互依存的。神经、内分泌、心血管、呼吸、肌肉和排泄系统彼此制约，并共同维持稳态。一个好的画面是：稳态不是某个房间里的一个恒温器，而是一整栋楼里布满了恒温器——体温、血糖、血压、pH、体液平衡、氧气和二氧化碳水平——它们各自守着自己的设定点，同时不断被运动、食物和天气推来推去，而校正它们的正是你已经知道的同一套系统：神经负责又快又局部的校正，激素负责又慢又广泛的校正。'},
{en:'Why are nerves fast and hormones slow? It comes down to how the message travels. A nerve impulse is a physical wave of electricity moving down a cable — the speed is limited only by how fast that wave can travel and how far the cable is. A hormone has to be released into blood, survive the journey, and diffuse out of the capillary at the far end. Blood moves at roughly one metre per second at rest, so the delivery time is essentially the distance the blood has to travel. That is why a hormone can reach a fingertip in under a second but an organ deep in the abdomen takes longer, and why the whole system is inherently slower than a wire.',
zh:'为什么神经快、激素慢？这取决于信息传递的方式。神经冲动是沿着电缆向下传播的一股电能波，速度只受这股波本身传播速度和电缆长度限制。而激素必须先被释放到血液里，在旅途中存活，再从远端的毛细血管扩散出来。血液在静息时大约以每秒一米的速度流动，所以送达时间本质上就是血液要走的距离。这就是为什么激素不到一秒就能到达指尖，而位于腹部深处的器官需要更久，也为什么这套系统在本质上必然比一根电线慢。'},
{en:'Now zoom out to the whole athlete. Every time a muscle works it needs oxygen delivered and carbon dioxide removed. The heart and lungs must respond within seconds, and the respiratory system must actually change its own settings — faster and deeper breathing, or slower and shallower — rather than waiting to be told what to do. Meanwhile the digestive system is not busy during a race, which is exactly why the parasympathetic branch quietly pulls back on digestion during effort and lets it resume in recovery. The systems even take turns being the priority.',
zh:'再把视角拉远到整位运动员。每次肌肉工作都需要送来氧气、运走二氧化碳。心肺必须在几秒内作出反应，呼吸系统必须真正改变自己的设置——呼吸加快加深，或减慢变浅——而不是等着被告知该怎么做。与此同时，消化系统在比赛期间并不忙碌，这正是副交感分支在用力期间悄悄收回对消化的控制、并在恢复期让它重新工作的原因。这些系统甚至会轮流成为“优先事项”。'},
{en:'Different systems are tuned for different jobs, and the body shifts the balance depending on the demand. At rest and in recovery, digestion and repair matter more. During exercise, delivery of oxygen and removal of waste matter more. That re-prioritising is the whole point of inter-system communication: one change in the environment, such as the starter’s gun or a sudden rise in body temperature, triggers a rapid protective response and a slower sustained adjustment at the same time.',
zh:'不同的系统为不同的工作而调整，身体会根据需求改变平衡。静息和恢复时，消化与修复更重要；运动时，氧气输送和废物清除更重要。这种重新排定优先级，正是系统间通讯的意义所在：环境中的一次变化，比如发令枪响或体温突然上升，会同时触发一个快速保护反应和一个缓慢而持久的调整。'}],
numbers:{en:'Numbers to keep in your pocket. Blood flow to skeletal muscle at rest is roughly 20% of cardiac output; during maximal exercise it can rise to around 80–85%. Heart rate at rest is about 60 beats per minute, and 190 or more in a maximal effort. Blood travels at roughly 1 metre per second at rest, which is the basic reason hormone delivery is slower than nerve conduction. Oxygen delivery to the working muscle is the number that explains why training the aerobic system improves the phosphagen and glycolytic systems too — you cannot extract what you never delivered.',
zh:'需要记在脑子里的数字。静息时骨骼肌的血流约占心输出量的20%，最大运动时可达80–85%。静息心率约60次每分钟，最大努力时190次或更多。静息时血液流速大约每秒1米，这正是激素送达比神经传导慢的基本原因。送到工作肌肉的氧气量解释了为什么训练有氧系统也会改善磷酸原和糖酵解系统——你无法提取从未送来的东西。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Each system works alone and the brain coordinates them." The brain coordinates, yes, but the systems also constrain and depend on each other chemically and physically all the time. The heart cannot pump what the lungs have not oxygenated.',
zh:'“每个系统各自工作，由大脑来协调。”大脑确实协调，但系统之间也一直在化学和物理上互相依赖、互相制约。肺没有氧合的东西，心脏也无从泵出。'},
{en:'"Nerves are fast and hormones are slow, so hormones are unimportant in sport." Slow is not the same as unimportant. Adrenaline, insulin and growth hormone all matter enormously; they act on a longer timescale and over a wider area.',
zh:'“神经快、激素慢，所以激素在运动中不重要。”慢不等于不重要。肾上腺素、胰岛素和生长激素都非常重要，只是它们作用的时间尺度和范围不同。'},
{en:'"Only two systems count: nervous and endocrine." The cardiovascular, respiratory, muscular and excretory systems are the ones being regulated. Treating them as bystanders loses the whole idea of inter-system communication.',
zh:'“只有两个系统算数：神经和内分泌。”心血管、呼吸、肌肉和排泄系统正是被调控的对象。把它们当成旁观者，就丢掉了“系统间通讯”这个概念的全部意义。'}],
why:{en:'This is where a sports scientist earns their degree. Explaining why a runner needs to taper, why an altitude camp works, why a trained athlete recovers faster, or why a heatwave kills a cyclist all come down to the same idea: two or more systems pushing the same variable in the same direction until something has to give. When you can name the systems, the direction each one is pushing and the timescale it works on, you can explain almost any performance question the paper sets.',
zh:'体育科学学位正是在这里考出来的。为什么要减量训练、高原训练为什么有效、训练有素的运动员为什么恢复更快、为什么高温会拖垮一名骑行者——这些问题的答案都是同一个概念：两个或更多系统朝同一个方向推同一个变量，直到某个环节撑不住。当你能说出是哪些系统、各自往哪个方向推、以及作用的时间尺度，试卷上几乎所有运动表现类的问题你都能解释。'}},

'Feedback and integrated examples': {
metaphor:{en:'Your body is full of smoke alarms. Most of them work by negative feedback: the alarm senses smoke, and instead of making more smoke, it switches on the sprinklers and the room goes back to normal. That is negative feedback — the response undoes the change. But a smoke alarm is also wired into a fire drill. Once the alarm is triggered, the building does not calmly return to normal. It escalates, on purpose, until the fire is out. That is positive feedback — the response makes the change bigger, and it is not a fault, it is the design.',
zh:'你的身体里装满了烟雾报警器。大部分靠负反馈工作：报警器感知到烟雾后，不是制造更多烟，而是打开喷淋，让房间恢复正常。这就是负反馈——反应抵消了变化。但报警器也接着消防演练。一旦报警触发，整栋楼不会平静地回到正常，而是会有意升级，直到火被扑灭。这就是正反馈——反应让变化变大，这不是故障，而是设计。'},
walk:[
{en:'Negative feedback is the most common control mechanism in the body, and it is the default. It counteracts a change and returns the variable toward its normal range. When body temperature rises, temperature-sensitive receptors in the skin and the hypothalamus detect it, and the response is sweating and widening of skin blood vessels, which pushes heat out. When it falls, the response is shivering and narrowing of skin blood vessels, which keeps heat in. Crucially, the response stops as soon as the variable is back in range. That switching off is the defining feature of negative feedback, and it is what stops a fever from cooking you.',
zh:'负反馈是身体中最常见的调控机制，也是默认模式。它抵消变化，把变量拉回正常范围。当体温上升时，皮肤和下丘脑中的温度感受器检测到变化，反应是出汗和皮肤血管扩张，把热量推出去；当体温下降时，反应是发抖和皮肤血管收缩，把热量留住。关键在于，一旦变量回到正常范围，反应就会停止。正是这种“会关掉”的特性构成了负反馈的定义，也正是它让发烧不会把你煮熟。'},
{en:'Positive feedback is rarer, and it has a specific job: it amplifies a change until a particular endpoint is reached. Blood clotting is the standard example. A small cut damages a vessel wall, platelets gather at the site, and the clotting factors they release attract more platelets, which release more factors. The loop is self-amplifying. It runs until the clot seals the damage, and then it stops — but the endpoint is the point. Without positive feedback you would never stop bleeding. Childbirth is the other classic example: contractions trigger hormones that strengthen contractions, until the baby is delivered.',
zh:'正反馈较少见，它有一个明确的职责：把变化放大，直到达到某个特定终点。凝血就是标准例子。血管壁有一处小伤口，血小板聚集到损伤处，它们释放的凝血因子又吸引更多血小板，后者释放更多因子。这个循环是自我放大的。它一直运行到血凝块封住损伤处才停止——但重点正在于那个终点。没有正反馈你就永远止不住血。分娩是另一个经典例子：宫缩引发激素，激素加强宫缩，直到胎儿娩出。'},
{en:'The integrated examples are where this stops being abstract. In thermoregulation during a hot match, temperature receptors detect the rise, the control centre compares it against the set point, the effectors — sweat glands and skin blood vessels — act, and water is conserved through the antidiuretic hormone. The nervous system handles the immediate correction; the hormonal system handles the slower conservation of fluid. One event, two systems, two timescales.',
zh:'把知识整合起来，抽象概念就落地了。在炎热比赛中的体温调节：温度感受器检测到上升，控制中心把它与设定点比较，效应器——汗腺和皮肤血管——开始工作，同时抗利尿激素保存水分。神经系统处理即时校正，激素系统处理更慢的保水。同一件事，两个系统，两种时间尺度。'},
{en:'The fight-or-flight response is the other integrated example. Sympathetic nervous activity and adrenaline together raise heart rate, breathing rate, muscle blood flow and available energy, all at once, in response to one stimulus. The body is not choosing between systems here. It is calling both, on purpose, because a threat needs an immediate response and a sustained one. Every part of that sentence is examinable, and the ability to use it on an unfamiliar example is exactly what the command word Apply is asking for.',
zh:'战斗或逃跑反应是另一个整合例子。交感神经活动与肾上腺素一起，同时提高心率、呼吸频率、肌肉血流量和可用能量，共同回应同一个刺激。身体在这里并没有在系统之间做选择，而是有意同时调用两个，因为威胁既需要即时反应，也需要持续反应。这句话的每个部分都可以考，而能否把这个框架用在一个陌生的例子上，正是命令词“应用”所要求的能力。'}],
numbers:{en:'Work through a real thermoregulation chain with numbers, because that is the shape the exam likes. A runner starts at 37 °C. In a hot race, core temperature climbs by about 1–1.5 °C before the correction catches up. Sweat rate in a hot endurance event can reach 1–2 litres per hour, and the guidance in the workbook is that losing more than about 2% of body mass through sweat is the threshold to act on. For a 60 kg athlete, 2% is 1.2 kg of fluid. That is why drinking to a plan, not to thirst, is the practical skill this section is really teaching.',
zh:'用数字走一遍真实的体温调节链，因为考试就喜欢这种形式。跑者起始体温37°C。在炎热比赛中，核心体温会上升约1到1.5°C，校正才追得上。高温耐力事件的出汗率可达每小时1到2升，而工作册给出的指导是：因出汗损失超过体重的约2%就是需要行动的阈值。对一位60公斤的运动员来说，2%就是1.2公斤水分。这正是为什么“按计划喝水”而不是“渴了才喝”才是这一节真正想教给你的实用技能。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Negative feedback means the body stops the change." It means the body opposes the change until the variable returns to range, then switches off. The change itself happened; the response undoes it.',
zh:'“负反馈就是身体阻止变化。”它的意思是身体对抗变化直到变量回到范围，然后关闭。变化本身已经发生了，反应把它抵消掉。'},
{en:'"Positive feedback is a fault in the body." It is a designed mechanism with an endpoint. Clotting and childbirth both fail without it.',
zh:'“正反馈是身体的故障。”它是一个有终点的设计好的机制。没有正反馈，凝血和分娩都会失败。'},
{en:'"One change in the environment can only produce one response." The whole point of inter-system communication is that one stimulus can trigger a fast response and a slow response at the same time. Temperature rise gives you sweating and ADH release.',
zh:'“环境的一次变化只能产生一个反应。”系统间通讯的全部意义正在于，一个刺激可以同时触发一个快反应和一个慢反应。体温上升会带来出汗和抗利尿激素释放。'}],
why:{en:'Feedback is what makes an athlete adaptable rather than merely reactive. A body that only reacted once would be helpless the second time a condition changed. Instead, every training session, every acclimatisation and every taper teaches the control systems a slightly better setting, which is the physiological reason training works at all. When a question asks you to justify a training change, the answer is almost always a variable, a receptor, a control centre, an effector and a feedback loop.',
zh:'反馈机制让运动员变得有适应力，而不只是被动反应。一个只会反应一次的身体，在条件第二次改变时就无能为力了。相反，每一次训练、每一次气候适应、每一次减量，都在教控制系统一个稍好的设定点——这正是训练之所以有效的生理原因。当题目要你论证一项训练调整时，答案几乎总是这样一条链：变量、感受器、控制中心、效应器、一个反馈回路。'}},

'Voluntary movement and reflexes': {
metaphor:{en:'Every movement you make on purpose goes through a phone call with the control room. You decide to jump, your brain calls your leg muscles, and they obey. But some things must not wait for a phone call. If something hot touches your hand, waiting for your brain to weigh up the options would be far too slow. So instead a shortcut wire runs straight from the skin to the spinal cord and back to the muscle, with no call to the brain at all. It is a reflex, and the reason it exists is simple: speed beats politeness when you are being burned.',
zh:'你所有有意识做出的动作，都要先给控制室打一通电话。你决定跳，脑就打电话给腿部肌肉，肌肉服从。但有些事不能等电话。如果有东西很烫碰到你的手，等大脑权衡利弊再决定就太慢了。所以有一条捷径线路直接从皮肤连到脊髓，再回到肌肉，完全不打电话给脑。它就是反射，存在的理由很简单：当你被烫到时，速度比礼貌更重要。'},
walk:[
{en:'Voluntary movement runs through four stages, and examiners like them in this order. First, sensory input: receptors detect something — light, sound, touch or position. Second, central processing: the brain and spinal cord interpret that information. Third, motor output: motor neurons carry the instruction out to the muscles or glands. Fourth, effector response: the muscle or gland actually does something. If you can name all four in order for an unfamiliar example, you have answered most of the Trace and Sequence questions in the paper.',
zh:'随意运动分四个阶段，考试官喜欢按这个顺序。 第一，感觉输入：感受器检测到某种东西——光、声、触觉或位置。第二，中枢处理：脑和脊髓解释这些信息。第三，运动输出：运动神经元把指令送到肌肉或腺体。第四，效应器反应：肌肉或腺体真正做出动作。如果你能在陌生例子中按顺序说出这四步，卷面上大多数“追踪”和“排序”题就答出来了。'},
{en:'Reaction time is the gap between the stimulus appearing and the movement starting, and it is measurable, which makes it examinable. Visual reaction time is typically around 200–250 milliseconds; auditory is faster, around 140–180 milliseconds, because sound reaches the brain slightly sooner than light does. Anticipation is what a trained athlete actually does in competition: they start before the stimulus arrives, which is why sprinters in the blocks are already moving at the gun rather than reacting to it. Reacting is what untrained players do, and it costs about a fifth of a second.',
zh:'反应时间是刺激出现到动作开始之间的间隔，它是可测量的，所以可考。视觉反应时间通常约200到250毫秒；听觉更快，约140到180毫秒，因为声音到达大脑略早于光。预判才是训练有素的运动员在比赛中真正做的事：他们在刺激到来之前就启动了，这就是为什么起跑器上的短跑运动员在枪响时已经在动，而不是对枪声作出反应。作出反应的是未经训练的选手，而他们要付出大约五分之一秒的代价。'},
{en:'Reflexes are fast, involuntary and protective, and the spinal cord is in charge. The knee jerk is the textbook example: the tendon under the kneecap is stretched, the stretch receptors in the muscle fire, the information goes straight to the spinal cord, motor neurons respond, the quadriceps contracts and the lower leg swings forward — all before the brain has registered that anything happened. A reflex is not a smaller version of a voluntary movement. It is a different circuit, designed for a different job.',
zh:'反射快速、不受意志控制、具有保护性，并且由脊髓负责。膝反射是教科书例子：膝盖下方的肌腱被牵拉，肌肉中的牵张感受器兴奋，信息直接进入脊髓，运动神经元作出反应，股四头肌收缩，小腿向前摆动——这一切都发生在大脑意识到发生了什么事之前。反射不是随意运动的“缩小版”，它是另一套线路，为另一种任务而设计。'},
{en:'The spinal cord is also smart enough to know when not to answer. If a muscle is already contracted, stretching it further would risk tearing it, so the circuit reduces its own response. This is called reciprocal inhibition: the muscle that must relax is deliberately switched off at the same time as the muscle that contracts. It is the reason a knee jerk is a smooth movement rather than a spasm, and it is a detail that separates a strong answer from a correct one.',
zh:'脊髓还聪明到知道什么时候不该回应。如果一块肌肉已经收缩，再进一步牵拉就有撕裂风险，于是这个回路会降低自己的反应强度。这叫交互抑制：应该放松的那块肌肉，会在收缩肌工作的同时被主动关闭。这正是膝反射是一个平滑动作而不是痉挛的原因，也是把“正确”答案提升为“优秀”答案的一个细节。'},
{en:'Finally, reflexes do not stop at the knee. Walking, swallowing, breathing, blinking and the withdrawal of a hand from a hot surface are all spinal reflexes running in everyday life. They are not things you decided to do. They are the default wiring that you do not have to think about, which is exactly what makes them reliable under pressure.',
zh:'最后，反射也不止于膝反射。行走、吞咽、呼吸、眨眼，以及把手从热物体上缩回，都是日常生活中的脊髓反射。它们不是你决定去做的事，而是你不必思考的默认线路——这正是它们在压力下依然可靠的原因。'}],
numbers:{en:'The numbers here are small and worth memorising. Visual reaction time is about 200–250 ms; auditory reaction time about 140–180 ms; the total time for a simple reflex arc such as the knee jerk is about 30–50 ms, because it never reaches the brain. Put those three side by side and the design principle becomes obvious: the spinal cord is roughly four times faster than seeing and reacting on purpose. That gap is the entire reason reflexes exist.',
zh:'这一节的数字很小，值得记住。视觉反应时间约200到250毫秒；听觉反应时间约140到180毫秒；像膝反射这样一条简单反射弧的完整时间约30到50毫秒，因为它根本没到脑。把三个数字并排看，设计原理就一目了然：脊髓比“看到之后有意识地反应”快大约四倍。这个差距就是反射存在的全部理由。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"A reflex is a very fast voluntary response." It is not voluntary and it is not a response you chose. Involuntary and fast is the whole point; voluntary would make it useless in an emergency.',
zh:'“反射是一种非常快的随意反应。”它既不随意，也不是你选择的反应。不受意志控制而且快，这正是它的意义；如果变成随意的，在紧急情况就没有用了。'},
{en:'"All reflexes are processed in the brain." Most protective reflexes are integrated in the spinal cord. The brain is told afterwards, if at all.',
zh:'“所有反射都在大脑处理。”大多数保护性反射是在脊髓中整合的。大脑要么之后才被告知，要么根本不会被告知。'},
{en:'"Reaction time is how fast you move." Reaction time is the delay before you start moving. Movement speed is a separate thing, and confusing them costs marks.',
zh:'“反应时间就是你动作有多快。”反应时间是你开始动之前的延迟。动作速度是另一回事，混淆这两者会丢分。'}],
why:{en:'Reaction time is a real, trainable, measurable number in sport, and it is one of the few physiological things where a beginner and an elite differ by a large, obvious margin. A goalkeeper who reads a penalty earlier by 50 milliseconds has a genuine physical advantage. This is also where the Apply command word earns marks: a sprinter using a block start, a netballer anticipating a pass, and a goalkeeper setting themselves are all the same principle — using prediction instead of reaction, because prediction is free and reaction always costs you time.',
zh:'反应时间在运动中是真实、可训练、可测量的数字，也是少数几个新手与顶尖选手差距巨大且明显的生理指标之一。一个能早50毫秒判断出点球的守门员，拥有的是实实在在的物理优势。这也是命令词“应用”最容易得分的地方：短跑运动员用起跑器、无板篮球运动员预判传球、守门员提前站位——都是同一个原理，用预判代替反应，因为预判是免费的，而反应永远要付出时间。'}},

'Hormonal influences and sport applications': {
metaphor:{en:'Nerves are a text message. Hormones are a letter that arrives by post. The text arrives first and is specific: it goes to one phone. The letter travels slowly, reaches everyone in the house, and changes the mood in the whole house. In sport that means a hundredth of a second can come from your nerves, but a change that lasts an hour — how much energy your body is willing to spend today, how much it is willing to repair tonight — comes from hormones.',
zh:'神经像一条短信，激素像一封走邮寄来的信。短信先到而且精准：它只发到某一部手机。信走得很慢，送到屋子里的每个人那里，改变的是整间屋子的情绪。在运动中这就意味着：百分之一秒的反应来自神经，而持续一小时的改变——你的身体今天愿意投入多少能量、今晚愿意修复多少——来自激素。'},
walk:[
{en:'The nervous system supports timing, precision and correction. It decides when a muscle fires and how hard, and it corrects errors within milliseconds. The hormonal system does something different: it changes how the body is set up over minutes, hours and days. Before a race, adrenaline raises heart rate, redirects blood to the muscles and releases glucose so that fuel is available. None of that is an accident of the moment; it is a deliberate change of the body’s settings, made in advance, and it cannot be undone by willpower.',
zh:'神经系统负责时机、精度和校正：它决定肌肉何时收缩、收缩多大力，并在几毫秒内纠正错误。内分泌系统做的则不同：它在几分钟、几小时和几天的时间尺度上改变身体的配置。比赛前，肾上腺素提高心率、把血液重新分配到肌肉并释放葡萄糖，使燃料到位。这些都不是偶然发生的，而是对身体设定值有意做出的调整，事先就完成了，而且无法靠意志力撤销。'},
{en:'The major stress hormones are adrenaline and cortisol. Adrenaline is the acute one. Cortisol is the chronic one: it raises available glucose, which is useful, but if it stays high for long enough it promotes protein breakdown, which is exactly the wrong thing for an athlete trying to build or repair tissue. This is the clearest example on the whole course of why "more" is not always "better" — a hormone that is essential in a short emergency can be destructive in a long-term situation.',
zh:'主要的应激激素是肾上腺素和皮质醇。肾上腺素是急性的那一个；皮质醇是慢性的：它提高可用血糖，这很有用，但如果长期维持在高水平，就会促进蛋白质分解，而这对一个试图增加或修复组织的运动员来说恰恰是最不该发生的。这是整门课程中最清楚地说明“更多不等于更好”的例子——在短暂紧急情况中不可或缺的激素，在长期情境下可能是有害的。'},
{en:'The other hormones you need to know run on much longer timescales. Testosterone supports muscle protein synthesis, strength and power, and it is the reason resistance training changes the body structurally rather than just temporarily. Growth hormone supports growth and tissue repair, with much of its work happening during sleep. Insulin and glucagon between them keep blood glucose in a usable range — insulin moves glucose into cells after a meal, glucagon releases glucose from storage when fuel runs low. Oestrogen is relevant too: it contributes to bone health, and its cyclical change is one reason iron needs differ between people.',
zh:'其他需要了解的激素作用时间要长得多。睾酮支持肌肉蛋白质合成、力量和功率，这就是阻力训练能结构性改变身体、而不只是暂时改变的原因。生长激素支持生长和组织修复，其中很大一部分工作发生在睡眠期间。胰岛素和胰高血糖素共同把血糖维持在可用范围——胰岛素在餐后把葡萄糖移入细胞，胰高血糖素在燃料不足时把葡萄糖从储备中释放出来。雌激素同样相关：它有助于骨骼健康，其周期性变化也是不同人群铁需求存在差异的原因之一。'},
{en:'Notice how the two systems divide the work. Timing, precision and immediate correction are neural. Sustained change in capacity — how much power, how much you can repair, how readily you mobilise fuel — is hormonal. Sport is full of moments that need both: the first three seconds of a 100 m sprint are almost entirely nerve, and the adaptation that makes those three seconds faster next month is almost entirely hormone.',
zh:'注意两个系统是如何分工的。时机、精度和即时校正属于神经；能力的持续改变——力量有多大、能修复多少、能多快调动燃料——属于激素。运动中充满了同时需要两者的时刻：100米短跑的前三秒几乎完全是神经系统的工作，而让这三秒在下个月变快的适应，则几乎完全是激素的工作。'},
{en:'The boundary matters for exam answers. You do not need hormone concentrations, half-lives, receptor subtypes or the precise molecular pathway for any of these. You need to know which hormone is involved, what broad job it does, which system it belongs to, and give a real sport example where it is the reason something happens.',
zh:'边界对考点的答题很重要。这些内容你不需要知道激素浓度、半衰期、受体亚型或精确的分子通路。你需要知道的是：涉及哪种激素、它大致负责什么、属于哪个系统，并给出一个真实的运动例子说明它为什么导致某件事发生。'}],
numbers:{en:'Three numbers to keep. A maximal sprint runs about 10 seconds and is powered almost entirely by the phosphagen system, so the adrenaline that arrives after the first few seconds is too late to help the race itself — it helps the ten minutes after it. Bone is the slowest tissue to adapt and the fastest to lose, which is why load and recovery have to be managed over months rather than weeks. And a rough rule of thumb: resistance training produces measurable strength change over about 4 to 8 weeks, and bone density responds over 6 to 12 months. The fastest thing you can change in sport is nerve and skill; the slowest is bone.',
zh:'记住三个数字。全力冲刺约10秒，几乎完全由磷酸原系统供能，所以几秒后才到达的肾上腺素对比赛本身来说已经太迟——它帮助的是之后那十分钟。骨是适应最慢、流失最快的组织，因此负荷和恢复必须以数月而非数周为单位来管理。还有一个经验值：阻力训练大约需要4到8周产生可测量的力量变化，骨密度需要6到12个月才有反应。运动中你能最快改变的是神经和技能，最慢的是骨。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Adrenaline makes you faster at the start of the race." For a maximal sprint it arrives after the decisive seconds. Adrenaline matters most in sustained effort, in the recovery afterwards, and in the seconds-to-minutes window of a 400 m or 800 m.',
zh:'“肾上腺素让你在比赛开始时更快。”在全力短跑中，它到达时决定性的几秒已经过去了。肾上腺素在持续性项目、之后的恢复期，以及400米或800米那种几秒到几分钟的区间里才更重要。'},
{en:'"Growth hormone builds muscle." Its main job is growth and tissue repair, and much of it acts during sleep. For building muscle in a trained adult, resistance training and adequate protein are the levers; growth hormone is a supporting actor, not the star.',
zh:'“生长激素长肌肉。”它的主要职责是生长和组织修复，而且很大一部分在睡眠中起作用。对训练中的成年人来说，增肌的杠杆是阻力训练和充足的蛋白质；生长激素是配角，不是主角。'},
{en:'"Hormones are the slow system, so they do not matter in a fast sport." Slow is not the same as unimportant. The adrenaline that starts your sprint at 0.6 seconds, the growth hormone that repairs tissue overnight and the insulin that decides how fuel is handled after a meal are all slow, and all essential.',
zh:'“激素是慢的系统，所以在快的运动里不重要。”慢不等于不重要。让冲刺在0.6秒启动的肾上腺素、修复夜间组织的生长激素、以及决定餐后燃料处理的胰岛素，都很慢，也都必不可少。'}],
why:{en:'This section is the difference between explaining and guessing. When a question asks why a sprinter needs a good sleep, or why a teenage athlete is growing faster than an adult, or why tapering matters before a competition, hormonal knowledge lets you give the actual mechanism instead of a vague answer about rest. And because the paper is built on command words, the highest-value skill here is not memorising gland names. It is being able to take any hormone, describe its broad role, and link it to a specific moment in a specific sport.',
zh:'这一节是“解释”和“猜”之间的分界线。当题目问为什么短跑运动员需要充足睡眠、为什么青少年长得比成年人快、为什么赛前减量重要时，激素知识让你能给出真正的机制，而不是关于休息的含糊回答。而且由于试卷建立在命令词之上，这里最有价值的技能并不是背腺体名称，而是能对任何一种激素说出它的大致作用，并把它连到某个具体项目中的具体时刻。'}},

/* ─── A.2 · Water, fuelling and the energy systems ─────────────────── */

'Functions, intake and loss': {
metaphor:{en:'Your body is a swimming pool that is never empty and never still. Two taps fill it: what you drink, and the water inside the food you eat. Four drains empty it: sweat, urine, the water you breathe out, and water leaving in faeces. You are not managing a tank that you fill once. You are managing a pool that is being filled and drained every minute of every day, and health is the level staying inside a fairly narrow band.',
zh:'你的身体像一个永不枯竭、也从不静止的游泳池。两个进水口负责注入：喝下的水，以及食物中所含的水。四个出水口负责排出：汗液、尿液、呼吸中呼出的水，以及粪便带走的水。你管理的不是一个只需灌满一次的水池，而是一个每一分钟都在注入和排出的水池；健康就是水位始终留在一个相当窄的范围内。'},
walk:[
{en:'About 60% of an adult body is water. That sounds like a lot, and it has to be, because water is the medium in which almost everything happens. Every chemical reaction in the body is a reaction in water. Every nutrient is transported in water. Every joint is lubricated by water. And most importantly for sport, water is the only way the body can carry heat away from working muscle.',
zh:'成年人体内约有60%是水。这个比例听起来很高，而且必须这么高，因为水几乎是所有事情发生的介质。身体里的每一次化学反应都是在水中进行的；每一种营养物质都在水中被运送；每一个关节都由水来润滑。而对运动最重要的是：水是身体把热量从工作肌肉带走的唯一途径。'},
{en:'List what water actually does and it is easier to remember: it regulates temperature by sweating, it transports nutrients and waste, it lubricates tissues, it supports blood volume and therefore blood pressure, and it makes chemical reactions possible. Notice that four of those five are about moving or protecting something. Water is less a nutrient and more the medium everything else happens in.',
zh:'把水实际做的事列出来，就容易记住了：它通过出汗调节体温，运送营养和废物，润滑组织，支持血容量从而维持血压，并使化学反应成为可能。注意这五项中有四项与“运送”或“保护”有关。水与其说是一种营养素，不如说是其他一切发生其中的介质。'},
{en:'Electrolytes are the dissolved minerals that make water work. Sodium holds fluid in the right places and lets nerves fire; potassium is what muscle contraction and the heart rhythm depend on; chloride helps balance fluid and acid. Without them the water is there but the chemistry falls apart, which is why drinking plain water for hours on end can leave you worse off than not drinking — you dilute the very salts you needed.',
zh:'电解质是溶解在水中的矿物质，正是它们让水发挥作用。钠把液体维持在正确的位置，并让神经能够放电；钾是肌肉收缩和心律所依赖的东西；氯有助于维持体液和酸碱平衡。没有它们，水还在，但化学就乱了套——这正是连续数小时只喝白水可能让你状态更差的原因：你把本来需要的盐分稀释掉了。'},
{en:'The key idea is that fluid balance is a matching problem, not a willpower problem. You stay healthy when intake roughly equals loss, and you fall behind when losses exceed intake. In a cool room on a normal day, losses are modest and thirst is a good enough guide. In a hot race, losses can be large enough that thirst arrives far too late, which is why drinking to a plan matters more than drinking when you feel like it.',
zh:'关键在于：体液平衡是一个“匹配问题”，而不是“意志力问题”。当摄入大致等于流失时你保持健康，当流失超过摄入时你就开始落后。在凉爽房间里的普通一天，流失不大，口渴本身就是足够好的指引。而在炎热比赛中，流失可能大到口渴来得太晚——这就是为什么“按计划补水”比“想喝才喝”更重要。'}],
numbers:{en:'Numbers worth having in your head. Total body water is about 60% of adult body mass, so a 70 kg athlete carries roughly 42 litres. That water arrives as drinking and food, and leaves as roughly 1-2 litres of urine a day, a quarter to half a litre of sweat on an easy day, and a smaller amount through breathing and faeces. In the heat, sweat loss rises to about 0.5-2.0 litres per hour with a sodium loss of roughly 300-1000 mg per hour. The exam habit: put the two taps and the four drains on the same page, then explain any scenario by saying which drain opened and whether the tap kept up.',
zh:'需要记在脑子里的数字。成年人体内水分约占体重60%，所以一位70公斤的运动员体内约有42升水。这些水来自饮水和食物，而每天大约通过尿液排出1到2升、轻松训练日通过汗液排出0.25到0.5升，其余经呼吸和粪便排出。在高温环境下，出汗量上升到每小时0.5到2.0升，钠损失约为每小时300到1000毫克。备考习惯：把两个进水口和四个出水口写在同一张纸上，然后解释任何情境时都说明是哪个出水口被打开、进水口有没有跟上。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Water is just a drink you take after exercise." Water is the medium for temperature control, transport and every chemical reaction, and you need it before, during and after — not just after.',
zh:'“水不就是运动后喝的那杯水吗。”水是体温调节、物质运输和每一次化学反应的介质，你在运动前、进行中和之后都需要它——不只是之后。'},
{en:'"Electrolytes only matter in endurance events." Sodium, potassium and chloride are involved in nerve firing, muscle contraction and heart rhythm in every session, including a 100 m sprint.',
zh:'“电解质只在耐力项目中才重要。”钠、钾和氯参与每一次训练中的神经放电、肌肉收缩和心律，包括100米冲刺。'},
{en:'"Thirst tells you when to drink." On a normal day it is a reasonable guide. In a hot long event, fluid loss outruns the thirst signal, which is why plans exist.',
zh:'“口渴会告诉你什么时候喝水。”普通情况下它是合理指引。在炎热的长距离项目中，流失会跑在口渴信号前面，所以才有“计划”这回事。'}],
why:{en:'Every performance has a hidden variable, and in the heat it is fluid. Two per cent of body mass lost to sweat is the line the workbook uses, and for a 70 kg athlete that is 1.4 kg. Below it, performance is largely protected. Above it, core temperature climbs, the heart has to beat faster to move the same blood, the gut gets starved of flow, and the central nervous system begins to suffer, which shows up as worse decision-making and earlier fatigue. Drinking is not a chore attached to training. It is one of the variables that decides how the session goes.',
zh:'每一次表现背后都有一个隐藏变量，而在高温中它就是体液。2%的体重因出汗而下降是工作册给出的界线，对70公斤的运动员来说是1.4公斤。低于这条线，表现基本受到保护；高于它，核心体温上升，心脏必须跳得更快才能泵出同样多的血，肠道血流被剥夺，中枢神经系统开始受累——表现出来就是决策变差和更早疲劳。喝水不是训练附带的杂务，而是决定这次训练结果的一个变量。'}},

'Imbalance and hydration monitoring': {
metaphor:{en:'Checking hydration is like checking a car with one instrument on the dashboard. The oil light tells you something, the temperature gauge tells you something else, and neither one is the whole story. A good driver cross-checks. Body-mass change tells you how much fluid left, urine colour tells you roughly how concentrated it is, and urine osmolality tells you how hard the kidneys are working to hold water. Any one of them alone can mislead you.',
zh:'检查水合状态就像只靠仪表盘上的一个指示灯判断车况。机油灯说明一些事，水温表说明另一些事，而哪一个都不是全部。好的司机会交叉核对：体重变化告诉你流失了多少液体，尿色大致告诉你尿液有多浓，尿液渗透压则告诉你肾脏为了留住水分有多用力。单独依赖其中任何一个都可能误导你。'},
walk:[
{en:'Dehydration is simply a water deficit: you have lost more fluid than you have taken in. It is the mildest and most common of the three problems here, and it is also the one that a well-designed session should never produce. A 1-2% deficit sounds trivial, but it reliably raises heart rate at a given workload, raises core temperature, increases perceived effort and multiplies the risk of heat illness.',
zh:'脱水就是水分赤字：你流失的液体超过摄入的液体。它是这里三种问题中最轻、也最常见的一种，同时也是一个设计良好的训练根本不该产生的那种。1%到2%的赤字听起来微不足道，但它会可靠地让同等负荷下的心率上升、核心体温升高、主观用力感增加，并成倍放大热病风险。'},
{en:'Hypernatremia is too much sodium in the blood. It usually follows heavy sweating with too little fluid replacement, because sweat loses more water than sodium, so replacing the volume with plain water concentrates what is left. The picture is a runner who has sweated a great deal, has not drunk enough, and whose blood sodium has climbed. The fix is fluid with sodium in it, not more plain water.',
zh:'高钠血症是血液中钠过多。它通常发生在大量出汗而补液不足之后，因为汗液流失的水分多于钠，用白水补回体积只会让剩下的部分更浓。典型画面是：一位大量出汗、补水不足的跑者，血钠升高。解决办法是补含钠的液体，而不是更多白水。'},
{en:'Hyponatremia is the opposite: too little sodium in the blood. It is the one that surprises people, because it is caused by drinking too much rather than too little. If a runner drinks several litres of plain water over a long hot event and does not replace sodium, the blood sodium can be diluted below safe levels — and the symptoms, which include nausea, headache and confusion, look like dehydration. This is the single most useful thing to understand on this page, because the treatment is the opposite of what the patient wants.',
zh:'低钠血症正相反：血液中钠太少。它最让人意外，因为原因是喝得太多而不是太少。如果一位跑者在炎热的长距离项目中喝了好几升白水却没有补钠，血钠可能被稀释到不安全水平——而症状包括恶心、头痛和意识模糊，看起来非常像脱水。这是这一页最值得理解的一点，因为治疗方法和病人想要的恰好相反。'},
{en:'So how do you monitor it? The workbook guidance is to keep body-mass loss under 2% during exercise, and to use more than one indicator. Body-mass change is the most direct: weigh yourself before and after, nude and dried, and subtract. Urine colour is a crude but useful hydration marker, and it is a poor stand-in on its own because it is also affected by vitamins, medication and certain foods. Urine osmolality measures how concentrated the urine is, which reflects how hard the kidneys are working to conserve water.',
zh:'那么该怎么监测？工作册的指导是：运动中体重下降保持在2%以内，并使用一个以上的指标。体重变化最直接：运动前后称重，脱去衣物并擦干，然后相减。尿色是一个粗糙但有用的水合标志，单独使用并不理想，因为维生素、药物和某些食物也会影响它。尿液渗透压测量尿液浓度，反映肾脏为保存水分而工作的程度。'},
{en:'The exam-safe way to answer any monitoring question is to name at least two indicators, say what each one reports, and then say why one alone is not enough. That last clause is what separates a correct answer from a good one.',
zh:'回答任何监测类问题的稳妥方式是：说出至少两个指标，说明各自反映什么，然后解释为什么单独一个不够。最后这一点正是把“正确”答案变成“优秀”答案的关键。'}],
numbers:{en:'Do the arithmetic once so it is automatic. A 70 kg athlete who finishes a hot race 1.4 kg lighter has lost exactly 2% of body mass, and 1.4 kg of water is 1.4 litres. For sweat rates, 0.5-2.0 litres per hour over a two-hour race is 1 to 4 litres — which is why a two-hour road event in the heat can empty a normal water bottle several times over. And the sodium side of the same event: at 300-1000 mg per hour, a two-hour race costs 600-2000 mg of sodium, roughly a quarter to three-quarters of a teaspoon of salt. That is the number that justifies electrolyte drinks rather than plain water.',
zh:'把算式做一遍，以后就自动会了。一位70公斤的运动员在炎热比赛后轻了1.4公斤，正好是体重的2%，而1.4公斤水就是1.4升。出汗率方面，每小时0.5到2.0升，两小时就是1到4升——这就是为什么炎热环境下的两小时公路赛可能把你的水瓶喝空好几轮。同样这项运动的钠损失：每小时300到1000毫克，两小时就是600到2000毫克，约等于四分之一到四分之三茶匙盐。这正是选择含电解质饮料而不是白水的依据。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Urine colour tells you everything about hydration." It is one indicator among several, and it is confounded by vitamins, medication and food. Weight change and urine osmolality give complementary evidence.',
zh:'“尿色能说明水合的全部情况。”它只是若干指标之一，还会受到维生素、药物和食物的干扰。体重变化和尿液渗透压提供的是互补证据。'},
{en:'"Hyponatremia happens when you do not drink enough." It happens when you drink plenty but do not replace sodium. The cause is the opposite of dehydration.',
zh:'“低钠血症发生在喝得不够时。”它发生在喝得很多但没有补钠时。成因与脱水正好相反。'},
{en:'"Losing 2% of body mass is nothing." It is the point at which heart rate, core temperature, gut blood flow and central nervous system function all start to measurably deteriorate.',
zh:'“损失2%体重没什么大不了。”这正是心率、核心体温、肠道血流和中枢神经功能开始出现可测量恶化的分界点。'}],
why:{en:'Hydration monitoring is one of the few places where an amateur and a professional are separated by paperwork rather than talent. Weighing before and after, logging what was drunk, and recording urine colour takes about three minutes and it changes what you can conclude about a session. It is also the safest practical advice in this topic: a plan that tells you to drink to a number, and to include sodium when the session is long and hot, is a plan that has been shown to work repeatedly.',
zh:'水合监测是少数几个把业余者和职业者区分开的领域之一——靠的是记录，而不是天赋。运动前后称重、记录饮水量、记录尿色，大约花三分钟，却能改变你对这次训练能得出什么结论。这也是本主题中最安全的实用建议：按数字喝水，当训练时间又长又热时把钠也算进去——这样的方案已经被反复证明有效。'}},

'ADH and cardiovascular drift': {
metaphor:{en:'When your body is short of water it does two things at once, and it is worth keeping them apart. ADH is the water authority turning on a water-saving order: your kidneys stop sending water out in urine and start reabsorbing it. Cardiovascular drift is different — it is what happens to your engine when the coolant level drops, so the same job makes the machine run hotter and the fan has to spin faster.',
zh:'身体缺水时会同时做两件事，把它们分开理解很有价值。抗利尿激素（ADH）是水务局下达的节水令：肾脏不再把水随尿排出，而是开始重新吸收。而心血管漂移是另一回事——它是冷却液减少时发动机发生的事，同样的工作让机器跑得更热，风扇必须转得更快。'},
walk:[
{en:'The trigger is concentration, not volume. Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus sense the concentration of dissolved particles in the blood, which is osmolality. When it rises, meaning the blood is more concentrated than it should be, the hypothalamus tells the posterior pituitary to release ADH. ADH then acts on the kidney collecting ducts, increasing water reabsorption, so you produce a smaller volume of more concentrated urine. The body is not making water; it is keeping the water it already has.',
zh:'触发条件是浓度，而不是体积。下丘脑中的渗透压感受器感知血液中溶解粒子的浓度，也就是渗透压。当它升高，也就是血液比应有的更浓时，下丘脑会通知垂体后叶释放抗利尿激素。ADH 随后作用于肾集合管，增加水的重吸收，于是你排出的尿量更少、浓度更高。身体并没有制造水，而是不停地留住已有的水。'},
{en:'Sweating is the bigger loss. In the heat, sweat rate can reach 0.5-2.0 litres per hour, which is far more than most people replace. Blood volume falls, and blood volume is what fills the heart. A heart that is filling less completely has to beat faster to deliver the same amount of oxygen to the working muscle. That is the first half of cardiovascular drift: same pace, higher heart rate.',
zh:'出汗才是更大的流失。在高温下，出汗率可达每小时0.5到2.0升，远比大多数人补充的要多。血容量下降，而血容量正是填充心脏的东西。充盈不足的心脏必须跳得更快，才能把同样多的氧气送到工作肌肉。这就是心血管漂移的前半段：配速相同，心率更高。'},
{en:'The second half happens in the skin. As core temperature rises, more blood is sent to the skin to dump heat, and the skin is far from the working muscle. Two things follow: the muscle receives a smaller share of the available blood, and a larger share of the total is going to a non-muscle destination. So the working muscle must extract more oxygen from each unit of blood it gets. Both halves point the same way, which is why the numbers all rise together at the end of a long hot effort.',
zh:'后半段发生在皮肤。随着核心体温上升，更多的血液被送往皮肤散热，而皮肤离工作肌肉很远。于是出现两件事：肌肉从可支配血流中分到的份额减少，而总血流中更大的一份被送往非肌肉部位。因此工作肌肉必须从每单位血液中提取更多氧气。两半原因指向同一个方向，这就是为什么在长时间高温运动的末尾，这些数字会一起上升。'},
{en:'ADH cannot fix the whole picture, and it is worth being clear about that. ADH conserves water; it does not replace the volume you sweated, and it does not bring the sodium back. So the full recovery picture is three things together: replace the fluid, replace the sodium, and replace the water you need for the next session. Athletes often do the first and skip the other two, which is why the same athlete can be both dehydrated and low in sodium.',
zh:'ADH 无法解决全部问题，这一点值得说清楚。ADH 只能保存水分，它不能补回你出汗失去的体积，也不能把钠补回来。完整的恢复图景是三件事一起做：补回液体、补回钠、补回下一次训练所需的水分。运动员常常只做了第一件，跳过了另外两件——这正是同一位运动员可能既脱水又低钠的原因。'},
{en:'So a good answer to any drift question names the chain in order: sweat loss, falling blood volume, the heart compensating by beating faster, blood being redistributed to the skin, and the muscle extracting more oxygen from what is left. Each link causes the next. The exam word for this is a mechanism, and a mechanism is exactly what a list of symptoms is not.',
zh:'因此，任何漂移类问题的好答案都要按顺序说出这条链：出汗流失、血容量下降、心脏代偿性加快跳动、血液重新分配到皮肤、肌肉从剩余血液中提取更多氧气。每一环都引发下一环。考试里这叫作机制，而机制恰恰不是一串症状的罗列。'}],
numbers:{en:'The numbers make the drift concrete. Take a runner in the heat, working at the same speed. At the start, core temperature is near 37 degrees C and heart rate is, say, 150 beats per minute. Over an hour the core temperature may rise by 1-1.5 degrees and the heart rate for the identical pace may climb to 165 or even 175. Nothing about the pace changed. That gap of 15-25 beats per minute is cardiovascular drift, and the cause is on the thermometer and the scale, not in the legs. Sweat rate of 0.5-2.0 L/h and sodium loss of 300-1000 mg/h sit underneath both numbers.',
zh:'这些数字能让漂移变得具体。想象一位在高温中以同样速度跑步的运动员。开始时核心温度接近37摄氏度，心率比如是每分钟150次。一小时后核心温度可能上升1到1.5摄氏度，而同样配速下的心率可能升到165甚至175次。配速什么都没变。这15到25次每分钟的差距就是心血管漂移，其原因在体温计和体重秤上，而不在腿上。支撑这两个数字的，是每小时0.5到2.0升的出汗率和每小时300到1000毫克的钠损失。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"ADH makes you lose water." It does the opposite. ADH increases water reabsorption in the kidney, so you produce less, more concentrated urine.',
zh:'“抗利尿激素让你流失水分。”它恰恰相反。ADH 增加肾脏对水的重吸收，所以你的尿量更少、浓度更高。'},
{en:'"Cardiovascular drift means the athlete is going slower." It means the opposite: at the same pace the heart has to beat faster, so the athlete is working harder, not less.',
zh:'“心血管漂移意味着运动员变慢了。”它的意思正相反：同样配速下心脏必须跳得更快，所以运动员付出得更多，而不是更少。'},
{en:'"Drinking more fixes drift." Drinking replaces volume and sodium, which reduces the cause. But drift also comes from heat and from skin blood flow, so fluids alone do not remove it entirely.',
zh:'“多喝水就能消除漂移。”补液能补回体积和钠，从而减少诱因。但漂移还来自高温和皮肤血流，所以单靠液体并不能完全消除它。'}],
why:{en:'Cardiovascular drift is the best example on the course of a deceptively simple observation explaining a complicated result. A runner finishes a hot race with a higher heart rate at the same pace and a higher lactate, and it is easy to write that off as being unfit. In reality it is a predictable, well-understood response to a hot environment and an unfinished fluid plan. Recognising it means you can predict the outcome before the event — pacing, hydration and cooling all attack it — and that is the difference between knowing and guessing.',
zh:'心血管漂移是整门课程中最好的例子：一个看起来简单的观察，解释了一个复杂的结果。跑者在炎热比赛后以同样配速出现更高心率和更高乳酸，很容易被归因为“体能不够”。实际上它是对高温环境和未完成补液计划的、可预测且被充分理解的一种反应。认出它意味着你能在赛前预测结果——配速、补水和降温都在对抗它——这正是“知道”和“猜”的区别。'}},

'Macronutrients and individual needs': {
metaphor:{en:'Think of a car that runs on three different fluids. Petrol is the fast one: it burns quickly, it gives you power straight away, and if the tank is empty you cannot move. Oil is the slow one: it does not make the car go, but without it the engine wears itself out. And spare parts are the third one: the raw material the body uses to repair and rebuild. Ask what a car cannot do without each one and the answer is always the same: it cannot do anything at all without all three.',
zh:'把汽车想成靠三种不同液体运行的车辆。汽油是快的那一种：烧得快，立刻给你动力，油箱空了车就动不了。机油是慢的那一种：它不让车跑得更快，但缺了它发动机就会自己磨损。零配件是第三种：身体用来修理和重建的原料。问一台车缺了哪一样会怎样，答案总是同一个：缺了任何一样，它什么都做不了。'},
walk:[
{en:'Carbohydrate is the petrol. It is the main fuel for high-intensity exercise, and it earns that place for two reasons: it can be broken down quickly without oxygen, so it delivers ATP fast, and it spares protein, which means the body does not have to break down muscle to fuel itself. It also supports immune function and replenishes the glycogen stored in your muscles and liver, which is the reserve you draw on during a hard session.',
zh:'碳水化合物就是汽油。它是高强度运动的主要燃料，理由有两个：它可以在无氧条件下被迅速分解，因此能快速提供ATP；它还能节省蛋白质，意味着身体不必靠分解肌肉来供能。它还支持免疫功能，并补充储存在肌肉和肝脏中的糖原——那正是你在高强度训练中动用的储备。'},
{en:'Protein is the spare parts and the building material. It builds and repairs muscle and every other tissue, and it supplies the enzymes that run reactions, the hormones that signal, and the transport proteins that carry things around. This is the macronutrient with the most misunderstood job, because people think of protein as only being about muscle. It is about constantly replacing worn-out material, and that is happening in every tissue all day.',
zh:'蛋白质既是零配件，也是建筑材料。它构建和修复肌肉以及其他所有组织，并提供驱动反应的酶、发出信号的激素，以及运送各种物质的运输蛋白。这是功用最容易被误解的宏量营养素，因为人们以为蛋白质只和肌肉有关。实际上它关乎持续替换已经磨损的材料，而这在所有组织中、一整天都在发生。'},
{en:'Lipid is the oil, and it does three jobs that carbohydrate cannot. It is far more concentrated as an energy store, so it is the long-duration fuel. It helps protect organs, forming cushions around the heart and kidneys. And it is required to absorb vitamins A, D, E and K, because those vitamins dissolve in fat rather than water. Remove fat and you do not just lose fuel, you lose the ability to take up four vitamins.',
zh:'脂质就是机油，它承担着碳水化合物无法承担的三项工作。它作为能量储备要集中得多，因此是长时间项目的燃料；它保护器官，在心脏和肾脏周围形成缓冲层；它还是吸收维生素A、D、E、K所必需的，因为这四种维生素溶于脂肪而不溶于水。去掉脂肪，你失去的不仅是燃料，还有吸收这四种维生素的能力。'},
{en:'Needs are individual, and this is the part that scores. Children and adolescents need energy, protein, calcium and iron for growth. Menstruation raises iron requirements, which is why iron is specifically relevant to adolescent and female athletes. Beyond that, needs move with body size, lean mass, age and how much activity is actually being done. The exam-safe answer to "what should this athlete eat" is never a single universal diet. It is a list of factors, applied to one individual.',
zh:'需求因人而异，而这正是能得分的部分。儿童和青少年需要能量、蛋白质、钙和铁来生长；月经会提高铁需求，这也是铁与青少年及女性运动员特别相关的原因。除此之外，需求还会随体型、瘦体重、年龄以及实际活动量而变化。当题目问“这位运动员应该吃什么”时，稳妥的答案绝不是一份统一食谱，而是一份需要考虑的因素清单，然后应用到具体个人身上。'},
{en:'There is a second idea hiding here, and it is the one that separates a strong answer: energy availability. It is the energy left over for health and normal life after you have subtracted what training spent. The first question is not "did they eat enough calories" but "was there enough left over to stay well". An athlete can meet their energy needs for performance and still be in deficit for health, and that gap is where injuries, illness and stalled progress come from.',
zh:'这里还藏着第二个概念，也是把优秀答案区分出来的那个：能量可用性。它指的是扣除训练消耗之后，仍可用于健康和正常生活的能量。第一个问题不是“他们摄入的热量够吗”，而是“剩下的够维持健康吗”。一位运动员可能满足了表现所需的能量，却仍然在健康意义上处于赤字状态，而正是这个缺口带来了伤病、疾病和进步停滞。'}],
numbers:{en:'Numbers that let you reason rather than recite. Carbohydrate is the dominant fuel at high intensity, and general guidance is roughly 3-5 grams per kilogram of body mass per day for moderate training, rising to 6-8 g/kg for heavy endurance training. Protein sits around 1.4-2.0 g/kg per day for most athletes. Fat should rarely drop below about 20-30% of energy intake. All three are expressed per kilogram because needs track lean mass and body size, not just appetite. The habit worth building: when a question gives you an athlete, work out the training load first, then the energy the activity spends, then what is left over.',
zh:'让人能推理而不是背诵的数字。碳水化合物在高强度下是主导燃料，一般指导是中等训练每天每公斤体重约3到5克，重度耐力训练可到6到8克/公斤。蛋白质对多数运动员约为每天1.4到2.0克/公斤。脂肪通常不应低于总能量摄入的约20%到30%。三者都用“每公斤多少克”来表达，是因为需求跟瘦体重和体型有关，而不只是跟胃口有关。值得养成的习惯是：题目给了运动员的信息时，先算出训练负荷，再算出活动消耗的能量，最后看剩下多少。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Protein only builds muscle." It builds and repairs every tissue, and supplies enzymes, hormones and transport proteins. Muscle is the part people notice, not the whole job.',
zh:'“蛋白质只长肌肉。”它构建和修复所有组织，并提供酶、激素和运输蛋白。肌肉是人们注意到的部分，不是全部工作。'},
{en:'"Carbohydrate is only for endurance." It is the fastest fuel, so it matters most in short, intense events — the opposite of the popular belief.',
zh:'“碳水化合物只对耐力项目重要。”它是最快的燃料，所以在短时间高强度项目中才最重要，恰好与普遍看法相反。'},
{en:'"Low fat is always healthier." Fat carries vitamins A, D, E and K and protects organs. Too little fat is a real problem, and the useful number is a floor, not a ceiling.',
zh:'“脂肪越少越健康。”脂肪携带维生素A、D、E、K并保护器官。脂肪太少是真实的问题，而有用的数字是下限，不是上限。'}],
why:{en:'Fuelling is where nutrition most directly becomes performance. A carbohydrate-loaded muscle has bigger glycogen stores and lasts longer before it runs out. Adequate protein and energy availability are what let a muscle be rebuilt rather than merely broken down. And individualisation is not politeness: two athletes on the same training programme can need different food, and the difference between them is often exactly what decides who adapts and who breaks down.',
zh:'供能是营养最直接转化为表现的地方。糖原储备充足的肌肉能维持更久才耗尽。充足的蛋白质和能量可用性，让肌肉是被重建，而不只是被分解。个体化也不是客气话：两位执行同一训练计划的运动员可能需要不同的食物，而他们之间的差别，往往正是决定谁进步、谁垮掉的那个因素。'}},

'Pre-exercise, during-exercise and recovery': {
metaphor:{en:'Fuelling a training day works like preparing a journey. Before you leave you pack the boot: enough food for the trip, and nothing that will sit heavy and upset you. On the road you top up little and often rather than waiting until the tank is empty, because the engine is running on the way you feel now, not on what you will feel in an hour. Afterwards you do two jobs at once — refill what you spent, and repair what the journey wore out.',
zh:'为训练日供能，像准备一次出行。出发前你把后备箱装好：足够的食物，以及任何不会压在胃里让你不舒服的东西。在路上你少量多次地补充，而不是等到油箱空了才加，因为发动机消耗的是你此刻的状态，而不是一小时后的状态。结束后你要同时做两件事——补回花掉的，以及修复旅途损耗的。'},
walk:[
{en:'Before exercise, the guidance is 1 to 4 hours beforehand, carbohydrate-focused with some protein, low in fat and fibre, and with fluids taken in the same window. The reason for the timing is digestive: food eaten too close to the start is still sitting in the gut during the session, and gut blood flow is needed by working muscle. The reason for the composition is that carbohydrate is what you will burn, and protein is what you will need afterwards.',
zh:'运动前的指导是：赛前1到4小时进食，以碳水为主并搭配一些蛋白质，减少脂肪和纤维，同时在这个时间窗内补液。强调时间是为了消化：吃得太靠近开始，训练时食物还留在肠道里，而工作肌肉正需要肠道血流。强调成分是因为碳水是你马上要烧掉的，而蛋白质是你之后需要的。'},
{en:'During exercise, past about 60 minutes or at high intensity, the workbook recommends roughly 30 to 60 grams of carbohydrate per hour plus regular fluid intake. The mechanism is a supply problem: by the time you feel flat, the problem was created an hour earlier, when the rate of ATP use outpaced what your stores could give. Regular small amounts beat a large amount late, because the gut can only absorb so much at a time and unused carbohydrate in the gut is just weight you carry.',
zh:'运动中，超过约60分钟或强度较高时，工作册建议每小时补充约30到60克碳水，并规律补液。机制是一个供给问题：等你感觉“掉下去了”，问题其实在一小时前就造成了——ATP消耗速率已经超过了储备所能提供的。少量多次优于后期大量，因为肠道一次只能吸收有限量，而留在肠道里没被利用的碳水，只是你需要额外背负的重量。'},
{en:'Recovery has two jobs, and people usually only do the first. Replacing what you spent means fluid and sodium, and carbohydrate to refill glycogen. Repairing what the session wore out means protein. Timing matters most for protein: the muscles are most receptive for a few hours after training, so a meal with protein soon after is more useful than the same protein much later.',
zh:'恢复有两项工作，而人们通常只做了第一件。补回花掉的，意味着补液、补钠，并用碳水补充糖原。修复训练损耗的，意味着蛋白质。时机对蛋白质尤其重要：训练后几个小时肌肉最容易吸收，所以训练后不久就吃一顿含蛋白质的餐，比很晚吃同样多的蛋白质更有用。'},
{en:'Two practical habits sit on top of all of this. First, practise your fuelling in training: the gut adapts to what you give it and when, and nothing is worse than discovering a new gel at kilometre 30 of a race. Second, consider sodium for long or heavy-sweat sessions, and match replacement to actual sweat losses and conditions rather than using a fixed rule.',
zh:'在这一切之上还有两个实用习惯。第一，在训练中练习你的供能：肠道会适应你给它什么、何时给，而在比赛第30公里才第一次尝试某种能量胶，是再糟糕不过的。第二，对长时间或大量出汗的训练，考虑补钠，并且让补充量匹配实际汗液损失和条件，而不是套用固定规则。'},
{en:'And keep an eye on energy availability while you are doing all of this. Doing everything above and still not eating enough to cover the training is the route into low energy availability and RED-S. It is the failure mode that looks like dedication from the outside.',
zh:'在做这一切的同时，也要留意能量可用性。把上面所有事情都做对了，却仍然没有摄入足以覆盖训练消耗的能量，这就是通往低能量可用性和RED-S的路径。从外面看，这种失败模式看起来反而像自律。'}],
numbers:{en:'Do the numbers as a timeline. Pre-exercise: 1-4 hours, focused on carbohydrate, for example a familiar meal of rice, pasta, bread or fruit, with fluid taken in the same window. During: 30-60 g of carbohydrate per hour once the session passes about 60 minutes — a 60-minute race needs very little, a three-hour marathon needs most of that hour after hour. Fluid: replace a meaningful share of sweat loss, and in the heat reckon with 0.5-2.0 L per hour lost. After: protein within a couple of hours, with carbohydrate to refill glycogen and fluid and sodium to restore what you lost. A simple check that catches most problems: does the energy you took in cover the energy the session spent, plus enough left over to stay well?',
zh:'把数字当作一条时间线来用。运动前：1到4小时，以碳水为主，比如一顿熟悉的米饭、意面、面包或水果餐，同时在这个时间窗内补液。运动中：一旦超过约60分钟，每小时30到60克碳水——60分钟的比赛几乎不需要，三小时马拉松则需要一小时接一小时地补。补液：补回汗液损失中相当的一部分，高温下按每小时0.5到2.0升的流失估算。运动后：两小时内摄入蛋白质，同时用碳水补糖原、用液体和钠补回流失。一个能抓住大部分问题的简单自查是：你摄入的能量是否覆盖了这次训练的消耗，并且还剩下足够维持健康的？'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"The bigger the fuelling session, the better." Food eaten too close to start sits in the gut during the session, and gut blood flow belongs to working muscle. Timing is part of the plan.',
zh:'“吃得越多越好。”吃得太靠近开始，训练时食物还留在肠道里，而肠道血流属于工作肌肉。时间安排本身就是方案的一部分。'},
{en:'"Carbohydrate only matters in the marathon." It matters most per minute in short, intense work, where you run out of glycogen fastest. The 400 m runner is more carbohydrate-dependent than the marathon runner, per minute of racing.',
zh:'“碳水只在马拉松里重要。”按每分钟算，它在短时间高强度项目中最重要，因为那里的糖原消耗最快。每分钟比赛时间，400米跑者对碳水的依赖比马拉松跑者更大。'},
{en:'"Recovery is just eating more." Recovery has two jobs — replace what was spent and repair what was damaged — and the second one needs protein, on a timescale of a couple of hours.',
zh:'“恢复就是多吃一点。”恢复有两项工作——补回花掉的，修复损耗的——而第二项需要蛋白质，时间窗口是几个小时。'}],
why:{en:'Every one of these three windows is a decision you make before you know how the session will go, which is why fuelling belongs to planning rather than improvisation. The athletes who look effortless in a heat are usually the ones whose plan was written the night before and rehearsed in training, not the ones who reacted to thirst and hunger in real time. Practice is what makes the plan executable, and an executable plan is the whole advantage.',
zh:'这三个时间窗中的每一个决定，都是你在还不知道这次训练会怎样之前做出的——这就是为什么供能属于计划，而不是临场应变。那些在高温中看起来毫不费力的运动员，通常是计划在前一晚就写好、并在训练中演练过的人，而不是在当下对口渴和饥饿作出反应的人。练习让计划变得可执行，而可执行的计划就是全部优势所在。'}},

'Micronutrients, RED-S and microbiome': {
metaphor:{en:'Vitamins and minerals are the punctuation and the letters in a page of writing. Take all the words — carbohydrate, protein, fat, thousands of calories — and remove every full stop, every comma and every single letter, and you end up with a page of nothing but words. You would have all the volume and none of the meaning. That is what a micronutrient deficiency is.',
zh:'维生素和矿物质，是一页文字里的标点和字母。拿掉所有“词”——碳水、蛋白质、脂肪、几千大卡——再把每一个句号、每一个逗号和每一个字母都拿掉，最后剩下的只是一页没有标点的词。你会有全部的体积，却没有意义。微量营养素缺乏就是这个样子。'},
walk:[
{en:'Start with the four minerals that come up most. Iron builds haemoglobin, the molecule that carries oxygen in the blood, so low iron means less oxygen reaches the muscle and endurance suffers first. Calcium builds bone and teeth. Sodium supports fluid balance, blood volume, nerves and muscle. Potassium supports fluid balance and nerve and muscle function. All four are needed every day, in small amounts relative to the macronutrients.',
zh:'先从最常考到的四种矿物质说起。铁构建血红蛋白——血液中携带氧气的分子——所以缺铁意味着到达肌肉的氧气减少，耐力首先受影响。钙构建骨骼和牙齿。钠支持体液平衡、血容量、神经和肌肉。钾支持体液平衡以及神经和肌肉功能。这四样每天都需要，但相对宏量营养素而言量很小。'},
{en:'Then vitamins, which support immunity and the release of energy, with D and K especially relevant to bone health. The point to hold onto is that vitamin needs are small but not optional. A deficiency does not make you slightly worse at something; it produces a recognisable clinical picture, which is exactly why these are named and tested rather than being left vague.',
zh:'然后是维生素，它们支持免疫和能量释放，其中D和K与骨骼健康尤其相关。要抓住的要点是：维生素的需求量小，但并非可有可无。缺乏不会让你在某件事上稍差一点，而会产生一个可识别的临床图像——这正是它们被命名、被检测，而不是被含糊处理的原因。'},
{en:'Low energy availability is the condition where intake does not cover training expenditure, so there is not enough left for health. RED-S, relative energy deficiency in sport, is the name for what that does across systems. It can affect metabolism, menstrual function, bone mineral density, immunity, growth, cardiovascular and psychological health, recovery and performance. That list is long on purpose: the point is that under-fuelling is not a single problem but a whole-body one.',
zh:'低能量可用性是指摄入量无法覆盖训练消耗，因而没有足够剩下维持健康的情况。RED-S（运动中的相对能量缺乏）是这种情况在各个系统上造成后果的统称。它可以影响代谢、月经功能、骨矿物质密度、免疫、生长、心血管和心理健康、恢复以及表现。这个清单故意列得很长：重点在于，供能不足不是单一问题，而是全身性的问题。'},
{en:'The gut microbiome is a third angle, and it is the least examinable, so know its shape. Communities of microorganisms in the gut are influenced by genetics, diet, medication, sleep, stress and activity. Diet is the input you control, which is why a varied diet does more than supply nutrients — it feeds a population you did not choose and cannot see.',
zh:'肠道微生物组是第三个角度，也是最难考的，所以要掌握它的轮廓。肠道中的微生物群受到遗传、饮食、药物、睡眠、压力和活动的影响。饮食是你能控制的输入，所以多样化的饮食所起的作用不只是提供营养——它还在喂养一群你既没有选择、也看不见的生物。'},
{en:'Where exam answers usually go wrong here is generalisation. A safe, strong answer individualises: acknowledge the group where the issue is more common, name the mechanism that links it to the effect, and be clear that the fix is nutritional adequacy and individual monitoring rather than a supplement prescribed on principle. Iron for adolescent and female athletes is the standard worked example; calcium and potassium come from a varied diet.',
zh:'这一部分考题常错在“笼统作答”。稳妥而优秀的答案是个体化的：承认这个问题在哪些人群中更常见，说出把原因连到后果的机制，并明确解决办法是营养充足加个体监测，而不是凭原则开补充剂。青少年和女性运动员的铁是最典型的实例；钙和钾则来自多样化饮食。'}],
numbers:{en:'Numbers here are mostly about proportion rather than precision. Vitamins and minerals are needed in milligram or microgram amounts, which is why the daily values on a label look so small next to a carbohydrate figure in grams — and why "take a supplement" is almost never the answer to a dietary shortfall. The one figure worth carrying is the 2% body-mass fluid guideline, because low energy availability and dehydration often arrive together in the same athlete. Practically: a varied diet built on the macronutrient guidance supplies the micronutrients without calculation, and an athlete who is not eating enough to cover training is a candidate for professional assessment, not for a bigger pill.',
zh:'这一部分的数字更多是比例问题，而不是精确数值。维生素和矿物质的需求以毫克或微克计，所以标签上的每日值和以克计的碳水数字相比显得极小——这正是“吃补剂”几乎从来不是饮食不足的答案的原因。值得记住的一个数字是2%体重体液标准，因为低能量可用性和脱水常常同时出现在同一位运动员身上。实际做法是：建立在宏量营养素指导之上的多样化饮食，无需计算就能提供微量营养素；而如果一位运动员吃的不足以覆盖训练，他需要的不是更大的药片，而是专业评估。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Low energy availability is the same as not eating enough calories." It is not about total calories. It is about what is left after training expenditure, which can be inadequate even when calorie intake looks reasonable.',
zh:'“低能量可用性就是热量吃得不够。”它不是总热量的问题，而是扣除训练消耗后剩下多少的问题；即使总热量看起来合理，剩余也可能不足。'},
{en:'"RED-S is a nutrition deficiency you can fix with iron." It is a whole-body consequence of low energy availability. Iron may be part of the picture, but the underlying problem is the energy deficit.',
zh:'“RED-S 是补铁就能解决的营养缺乏。”它是低能量可用性造成的全身性后果。铁可能是其中一环，但根本问题是能量赤字。'},
{en:'"If body mass is normal, energy availability is fine." Body mass can look normal while energy availability is low, which is part of what makes RED-S easy to miss.',
zh:'“如果体重正常，能量可用性就没问题。”体重可以看起来正常而能量可用性却偏低，这正是 RED-S 容易被忽略的原因之一。'}],
why:{en:'This is the section that keeps athletes healthy over a career rather than merely fast for a season. The failure mode is not dramatic and it is not visible: an athlete trains harder each year, keeps performing, keeps getting injured, and never connects the two. Bone density, immunity, menstrual function and mood are all on that list, and all of them respond to the same fix — eating enough to cover training plus a margin for health. Recognising that is a professional skill, and it is why energy availability has moved to the centre of sports nutrition research.',
zh:'这一节决定的是运动员整个职业生涯的健康，而不只是某一个赛季的速度。问题既不戏剧化也不可见：一位运动员每年训练更重、成绩依然不错、伤病不断，却从未把这两件事联系起来。骨密度、免疫、月经功能和情绪都在这份清单上，而它们回应的是同一个办法——吃够训练消耗之外，还留出维持健康的余量。认出这一点是一项专业技能，也正是能量可用性成为运动营养研究核心的原因。'}},

'ATP and the energy continuum': {
metaphor:{en:'ATP is the only currency muscle can actually spend. Everything else is a way of getting more of it. Think of three different workshops all minting the same coin. The first is a small workshop with a fast printing press: it makes coins quickly but can only make a few at a time. The second is a bigger workshop, slower, making more. The third is a real mint, enormous and slow, but capable of supplying a whole city. Your muscles are supplied by all three at once, every minute, and only the proportions change.',
zh:'ATP 是肌肉真正能花出去的唯一货币，其他一切都是获取更多 ATP 的办法。把三个不同的车间想象成都在铸造同一种硬币。第一个是小车间配一台快速印刷机：出币很快，但一次只能造出少量。第二个是更大、更慢的车间，造得更多。第三个是真正的造币厂，庞大而缓慢，但足以供应整座城市。你的肌肉每一分钟都同时接受这三者的供应，变化的只是比例。'},
walk:[
{en:'ATP is the immediate energy currency. When it is hydrolysed to ADP and inorganic phosphate, energy is released, and that energy is what does the actual work: driving the cross-bridge cycle in muscle, pumping sodium and potassium across membranes, synthesising new molecules and firing nerve impulses. ATP is also astonishingly small and short-lived, which is why you cannot simply stockpile it and be done. Muscle stores only about 5 mmol per kilogram of wet muscle, which is a few seconds of maximal work.',
zh:'ATP 是即时能量货币。它水解为ADP和无机磷酸时释放能量，而这些能量才是真正做事的动力：驱动肌肉的横桥循环、把钠和钾泵过细胞膜、合成新分子、触发神经冲动。ATP 还小得惊人、寿命极短，所以不可能靠囤积它来解决问题。肌肉每公斤湿肌只储存约5毫摩尔——大约相当于几秒的最大用力。'},
{en:'Because the store is so small, three separate pathways exist purely to keep rebuilding it, and they run at the same time. What changes with intensity is not which one is on, but how much each one contributes. This is the single most misunderstood idea in exercise science, so it is worth being blunt: there is no switch. A person sprinting up a hill is using all three systems, and so is a person sitting still.',
zh:'因为储备如此之小，存在三条不同的通路专门用来持续重建它，而且它们同时运行。强度变化时改变的不是哪一条“开”，而是每一条贡献多少。这是运动科学里最常被误解的概念，所以必须直说：没有开关。正在上坡冲刺的人同时在用三个系统，坐着不动的人也一样。'},
{en:'The three systems differ on five axes, and holding those five axes is how you compare them properly: oxygen requirement, fuel, rate of ATP supply, how long they can last, and how quickly they recover. Power and duration are not opposites of each other. A system can be powerful without being sustainable, and that trade-off is the whole reason sport has events like the 100 m and the marathon rather than just one event.',
zh:'三个系统在五个维度上不同，握住这五个维度才能正确比较：是否需要氧气、燃料、ATP供应速率、能维持多久、以及恢复有多快。功率和持续时间并不是彼此的反面——一个系统可以有很高的功率却不持久，而这个权衡正是体育中既有100米又有马拉松、而不是只有一种项目的原因。'},
{en:'A worked example makes the continuum concrete. A 100 m sprint is dominated by the phosphagen system, a 400 m sprint gains substantial glycolytic contribution, and a 10 km run relies much more on oxidative resynthesis. But in all three cases the others are contributing, and the practical consequence is that training one system changes the others: a runner who trains the oxidative system becomes better at the phosphagen system too, because better delivery and better clearance are what let the fast systems do their job slightly longer.',
zh:'一个实例能让这条连续体变得具体。100米冲刺以磷酸原系统为主，400米冲刺大幅加入糖酵解贡献，10公里跑则更多依赖氧化再合成。但三种情况里其他系统都在参与，而实际后果是：训练一个系统会改变其他系统——一个训练了氧化系统的跑者，磷酸原系统也会变好，因为更好的输送和更好的清除，正是让快系统能多撑一会儿的条件。'},
{en:'Lactate deserves a specific word here, because it is badly misunderstood. It is a normal product of glycolytic metabolism, not a waste product and not the cause of fatigue by itself. It is a fuel your heart and other tissues can use, and its concentration rises when production outpaces clearance. Fatigue in a hard effort is driven by the whole picture — hydrogen ions, phosphate, depleted stores, heat and central fatigue — not by lactate on its own.',
zh:'乳酸盐在这里值得单独说一句，因为它被严重误解。它是糖酵解代谢的正常产物，不是废物，也不是疲劳的唯一原因。它是一种你的心脏和其他组织可以利用的燃料，而当生成速度超过清除速度时它的浓度会上升。高强度运动中的疲劳由整个图景驱动——氢离子、磷酸盐、储备耗尽、热量和中枢疲劳——而不是单靠乳酸盐。'}],
numbers:{en:'Numbers to fix in your head. Stored ATP is about 5 mmol per kilogram of wet muscle; phosphocreatine adds roughly three to four times that, giving the phosphagen system around 6-10 seconds of maximal effort. The glycolytic system dominates from roughly 30 seconds to about 2 minutes. The oxidative system takes over beyond that and can supply ATP for hours. The continuum therefore reads: seconds, then minutes, then hours. And the training consequence: five sets of 20 second maximal efforts with full recovery is the classic way to extend phosphagen capacity, because the adaptation comes from restocking phosphocreatine, not from the effort itself.',
zh:'要记牢的数字。储存的ATP约为每公斤湿肌5毫摩尔；磷酸肌酸大约是它的三到四倍，因此磷酸原系统大约提供6到10秒的最大用力。糖酵解系统大致主导30秒到2分钟。氧化系统从那之后接管，能供能数小时。所以这条连续体读起来就是：秒，然后分钟，然后小时。训练上的推论是：5组×20秒全力、组间充分休息，是延长磷酸原容量的经典方法，因为适应来自重新填满磷酸肌酸，而不是来自那次努力本身。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"One energy system switches off and another takes over." All three contribute at every intensity. What changes is the proportion each one contributes.',
zh:'“一个能量系统关闭，另一个接管。”在任何强度下三个系统都在贡献，变化的是各自贡献的比例。'},
{en:'"Lactate causes fatigue." Lactate is a normal product and a usable fuel. Fatigue comes from the combination of hydrogen ions, phosphate, depleted stores, heat and central fatigue.',
zh:'“乳酸盐导致疲劳。”乳酸盐是正常产物，也是可利用的燃料。疲劳来自氢离子、磷酸盐、储备耗尽、热量和中枢疲劳的共同作用。'},
{en:'"Training one system does not affect the others." It does. Better aerobic delivery and clearance let the fast systems work slightly longer, which is why endurance training also improves sprint capacity.',
zh:'“训练一个系统不会影响其他系统。”会。更好的有氧输送和清除让快系统能多撑一会儿，这就是耐力训练也会改善冲刺能力的原因。'}],
why:{en:'This framework is what lets an athlete and a coach make sense of training that otherwise looks contradictory. Why do a sprinter and a marathoner train so differently, when both are running? Why does lifting weights help a distance runner, and why does the weight room come with a sprint programme attached? Why does training make you feel worse before it makes you feel better? All of it falls out of the same five axes once you stop treating the systems as switches. Understanding the continuum is what turns a list of sessions into a plan with a reason behind it.',
zh:'正是这个框架，让运动员和教练能够理解那些看起来互相矛盾的训练。短跑运动员和马跑运动员都在跑步，为什么练得完全不同？举重为什么能帮到长跑运动员，而力量房里又总配着冲刺训练？为什么训练总是先让你感觉更差，之后才变好？只要你不再把这三个系统当成开关，这一切都会从同样的五个维度里自然得出。理解这条连续体，才能把一堆训练安排变成一个背后有理由的计划。'}},

'Comparing the three systems': {
metaphor:{en:'Three vehicles, three jobs. A rocket is the phosphagen system: astonishing power, about ten seconds of fuel, and then it is finished. A rally car is the glycolytic system: serious speed that can be sustained for a couple of minutes. A lorry is the oxidative system: slow off the line, but it can carry a load for hours on a small budget. No one argues about which is the best vehicle, because the question is always what the journey is for.',
zh:'三种交通工具，三种工作。火箭是磷酸原系统：功率惊人，大约十秒的燃料，然后就结束了。拉力赛车是糖酵解系统：速度相当可观，能维持一两分钟。卡车是氧化系统：起步慢，却能用很少的燃料把货运上几个小时。关于哪种车最好，从来没有争论，因为问题永远是：这趟路是干什么的。'},
walk:[
{en:'The phosphagen system uses ATP and phosphocreatine, needs no oxygen, produces very high power from a very small ATP store, and fatigues almost immediately. It is the system you are using when you jump out of the blocks, when you lift something heavy once, and when you throw. Think of the store as the money in your pocket on day one: it is small, it is immediately spendable, and there is no top-up during the effort.',
zh:'磷酸原系统使用ATP和磷酸肌酸，不需要氧气，从很小的ATP储备中产生极高的功率，并且几乎立刻疲劳。它是你冲出起跑器、一次举起重物、或者投掷时所用的系统。把这个储备想象成你第一天口袋里的钱：数量不大、立刻可用，而且在这次用力过程中没有补充。'},
{en:'The glycolytic system breaks down carbohydrate without oxygen, supplies a moderate amount of ATP at high intensity, holds that for roughly 30 seconds to 2 minutes, and produces lactate and hydrogen ions as by-products. Those hydrogen ions matter: they shift the pH of the muscle, and the proteins that generate force respond badly to that. This is the system behind 200 to 400 m running, repeated sprints and high-intensity interval training.',
zh:'糖酵解系统在没有氧气的情况下分解碳水化合物，在高强度下提供中等量的ATP，维持约30秒到2分钟，并产生乳酸盐和氢离子作为副产物。这些氢离子很关键：它们改变肌肉的pH，而产生力量的蛋白质对这种变化反应很差。这就是200到400米、重复冲刺和高强度间歇训练背后的系统。'},
{en:'The oxidative system requires oxygen, uses carbohydrate, fat and a little protein, supplies a large and steady amount of ATP, and can run for minutes to hours. It is the system that supports 1500 m, distance running, cycling and rowing, and it is also the system that makes all the recovery after a session possible.',
zh:'氧化系统需要氧气，使用碳水化合物、脂肪和少量蛋白质，提供大量而稳定的ATP，可以运行数分钟到数小时。它支撑着1500米、长跑、骑行和划船，同时也是训练后所有恢复得以进行的基础。'},
{en:'The comparison questions in the paper are always the same five: oxygen use, fuel, ATP supply, duration and recovery. Answering those five for each system, in that order, will always earn the mark. What loses marks is a paragraph that describes one system fluently and leaves the other two to inference.',
zh:'卷面上的比较题永远就是那五项：氧气使用、燃料、ATP供应、持续时间、恢复。把这五项按顺序为每个系统说一遍，标记就到手了。丢分的是那种把一个系统讲得头头是道、另外两个却留给读者去猜的段落。'},
{en:'The integrated point is the one that separates a good answer: a 400 m runner still gets aerobic support. There is no switch, so a 400 m effort is phosphagen power with heavy glycolytic contribution and meaningful oxidative contribution underneath. Pacing and training decide how long each system can keep contributing, which is why a paced 400 m and a raced 400 m are physiologically different despite identical distance and identical time.',
zh:'综合性的那一点正是区分好答案的关键：400米跑者仍然得到有氧支持。系统不是开关，所以400米的一次用力是磷酸原的功率、很重的糖酵解贡献，以及底下的实质性氧化贡献。配速和训练决定每个系统能维持贡献多久——这就是为什么同样距离、同样时间的400米，配速跑和拼力跑在生理上并不相同。'}],
numbers:{en:'A table you can build from memory. Phosphagen: no oxygen, ATP and phosphocreatine, very high power, about 6-10 seconds, fast recovery. Glycolytic: no oxygen, carbohydrate, moderate power, about 30 seconds to 2 minutes, recovery of roughly 10-20 minutes depending on how hard the session was. Oxidative: oxygen required, carbohydrate and fat, lower power, minutes to hours, recovery of hours to a couple of days. The two training consequences that follow: long, easy aerobic work raises the ceiling for how much you can deliver and how fast you clear things, and short, hard phosphagen work raises the ceiling for peak power. In a real programme you need both, and the ratio depends on the event.',
zh:'一张可以凭记忆搭出来的表。磷酸原：无需氧气，ATP和磷酸肌酸，功率极高，约6到10秒，恢复快。糖酵解：无需氧气，碳水化合物，中等功率，约30秒到2分钟，恢复约10到20分钟，取决于这次训练的强度。氧化：需要氧气，碳水化合物和脂肪，功率较低，数分钟到数小时，恢复从数小时到一两天。由此得到两条训练推论：长时间低强度有氧训练提高输送上限和清除速度；短时间高强度磷酸原训练提高峰值功率。真实的训练计划两者都需要，而比例取决于项目。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"Phosphagen is the most important system because it gives the highest power." Importance depends on the event. It is the whole answer in a 100 m and almost irrelevant in a marathon.',
zh:'“磷酸原功率最高，所以最重要。”重要性取决于项目。100米里它是全部答案，在马拉松里几乎无关紧要。'},
{en:'"Glycolysis produces lactate, so it is the anaerobic waste system." It is not waste. Lactate is a usable fuel for the heart and other tissue.',
zh:'“糖酵解产生乳酸，所以它是无氧的废物系统。”它不是废物。乳酸盐是心脏和其他组织可以利用的燃料。'},
{en:'"Each system has its own recovery time, so a 400 m and a 100 m train the same way." They do not. One is a phosphagen event needing high power and long rests, the other a glycolytic event needing a sustained pace.',
zh:'“每个系统都有自己的恢复时间，所以400米和100米的训练方式一样。”不一样。一个是磷酸原项目，要高功率和长休息；另一个是糖酵解项目，要维持配速。'}],
why:{en:'Knowing which system an event lives on tells you what training that event needs before a single session is programmed. A 100 m sprinter lives in the phosphagen system, so their programme is short, maximal and separated by long rests, because the adaptation is phosphocreatine restocking. A 5 km runner lives half in glycolysis and half in oxidative, so their programme is longer, more continuous, and rests are short. A shot putter and a road cyclist are the same distance apart and on opposite ends of the table, which is exactly why one blanket programme fails most athletes.',
zh:'知道一个项目活在哪个系统里，就能在排第一节课之前知道它需要什么训练。100米短跑运动员活在磷酸原系统里，所以计划是短促、尽力、组间长休息，因为适应来自磷酸肌酸的重新填满。5公里跑者一半活在糖酵解、一半活在氧化系统，所以计划更长、更连续、休息更短。铅球运动员和公路自行车手在体格上可能一样，却站在这张表的两端——这正是为什么一套统一计划对多数运动员都会失效。'}},

'VO₂max, movement economy, LIP and EPOC': {
metaphor:{en:'Four measurements that decide how far you can go. VO₂max is the size of the delivery van: how much oxygen can arrive per minute. Movement economy is how much fuel the courier must burn to carry one parcel, and a good courier barely notices the parcels. The lactate threshold is the point where the van starts to labour on the hill, breathing hard but still coping. EPOC is the engine running hot after you have switched it off.',
zh:'四个决定你能走多远的测量。最大摄氧量是送货车的大小：每分钟能送来多少氧气。动作经济性是快递员送一个包裹要烧多少油，而好的快递员几乎感觉不到包裹的存在。乳酸阈点是货车在爬坡时开始吃力的那个点——呼吸急促，但仍撑得住。运动后过量氧耗是熄火之后引擎仍在高温运转。'},
walk:[
{en:'VO₂max is the maximum rate of oxygen uptake, the ceiling on oxygen delivery. It is the strongest single predictor of endurance performance, and it is the reason an elite distance runner can hold a pace that would leave an untrained person walking. A typical range is about 3-5 litres per minute in trained men and 2-3 in trained women, and it is a ceiling, not a suggestion: if oxygen cannot arrive faster, the pace cannot be faster, no matter how motivated the athlete is.',
zh:'最大摄氧量是氧摄取的最大速率，也就是氧气输送的上限。它是预测耐力表现最强的单一指标，也正是精英长跑运动员能维持一个会让未训练者走不动的原因。训练良好的男性典型值约为每分钟3到5升，女性约2到3升。它是上限而不是建议：如果氧气无法更快到达，配速就不可能更快，无论运动员多有动力。'},
{en:'Movement economy is oxygen cost per kilogram of body mass per minute at a given speed, and it says nothing about fitness. Two athletes with the same VO₂max can be separated by economy. Better economy means the same speed at a lower oxygen cost, so there is more oxygen left in the spare capacity. Mechanism: a trained runner wastes less energy per step through stiffer tendons that return energy like a spring, a smoother stride, less unnecessary movement, and the ability to run at a lower VO₂max than an untrained person at the same pace.',
zh:'动作经济性是在给定速度下每公斤体重每分钟消耗的氧气，它与体能无关。两位最大摄氧量相同的运动员可以被经济性区分开。更好的经济性意味着同样的速度只用更少的氧气，因此还有更多余量。机制在于：训练有素的跑者每一步浪费的能量更少——肌腱更硬、像弹簧一样回弹、动作更流畅、多余动作更少，并且能在与未训练者相同配速下用更低的最大摄氧量完成。'},
{en:'The lactate inflection point is where blood lactate begins to rise above baseline. Below it, almost all of the work is aerobic; above it, anaerobic contribution climbs sharply. It is not a wall you hit, it is a region you enter, and the practical consequence is that pace selection is a physiological decision, not a preference. An athlete who can detect the threshold can sit just below it for a long time; one who cannot will drift above it and pay for it in the last third of the race.',
zh:'乳酸拐点是血糖乳酸开始高于基线的位置。在它以下，几乎全部工作都是有氧的；在它以上，无氧贡献急剧上升。它不是一堵撞上的墙，而是一片进入的区域，其实际后果是：配速选择是一个生理决定，而不是个人偏好。能察觉自己阈值的运动员可以在其下方维持很久；察觉不到的会漂到其上，并在比赛最后三分之一付出代价。'},
{en:'EPOC is the extra oxygen your body keeps consuming after you stop, above what the steady state would need. The fast component is roughly the first two minutes as heart rate falls and the most recent lactate is cleared. The slow component then runs on for a while, often hours, because the body is still paying off work: resynthesising phosphocreatine, reloading glycogen, shifting something toward fat, clearing heat and metabolites, and repairing tissue. It is the physiological reason a hard session is not over when you cross the line.',
zh:'运动后过量氧耗是指你停下来之后，身体仍然消耗的、超出稳态所需的额外氧气。快速成分大约是最初两分钟，此时心率下降、最近产生的乳酸被清除。之后的慢速成分会持续一段时间，往往长达数小时，因为身体仍在还债：重新合成磷酸肌酸、重新装载糖原、把供能向脂肪转移、清除热量和代谢物、修复组织。这就是为什么一次高强度训练在你冲过终点线时并没有结束。'}],
numbers:{en:'A few numbers that make these real. VO₂max in trained men is about 3-5 L/min and in trained women about 2-3 L/min. A good movement economy at marathon pace is roughly 5 mL of oxygen per kilogram per minute. The lactate inflection point for an elite endurance athlete sits around 1.6 mmol per litre of blood. And the recovery bill: EPOC after a hard 30 minute effort runs about 2 litres of extra oxygen, and if roughly a third of that goes to restoring glycogen, that is on the order of 90 grams of carbohydrate — which is why a hard session is followed by a carbohydrate-rich meal, not a protein shake and water.',
zh:'几个让这些概念落地的数字。训练良好的男性最大摄氧量约为每分钟3到5升，女性约2到3升。马拉松配速下良好的动作经济性约为每公斤每分钟5毫升氧气。精英耐力运动员的乳酸拐点大约在血糖1.6毫摩尔每升。恢复的账单：一次高强度30分钟训练后的过量氧耗大约多消耗2升氧气，若其中约三分之一用于恢复糖原，那就是大约90克碳水——这正是为什么高强度训练之后要吃富含碳水的正餐，而不是蛋白饮料加白水。'},
mistakes:[
{en:'"VO₂max is how hard you are working." It is a capacity ceiling, a measurement of the maximum, not of effort. The same athlete at three different intensities has one VO₂max and three very different heart rates.',
zh:'“最大摄氧量表示你多努力。”它是一个容量上限，是最大值的测量，不是努力的测量。同一位运动员在三种不同强度下只有一个最大摄氧量，却有三个截然不同的心率。'},
{en:'"VO₂max is a measure of fitness." It measures capacity, not skill or technique. An athlete can have a high VO₂max and still be slow because of movement economy.',
zh:'“最大摄氧量是体能的度量。”它衡量的是容量，不是技术或动作。运动员可能有很高的最大摄氧量，却因为动作经济性差而仍然很慢。'},
{en:'"Lactate is waste that has to be cleared before you can run again." It is a usable fuel. What is repaid after a hard effort is phosphocreatine, glycogen, heat clearance and tissue repair, which is why recovery takes hours and not minutes.',
zh:'“乳酸是废物，必须清空才能再跑。”它是一种可利用的燃料。高强度用力之后真正要还的是磷酸肌酸、糖原、热量清除和组织修复——这也是为什么恢复以小时计，而不是以分钟计。'}],
why:{en:'These four numbers are what turn a race plan into a physiological argument. An athlete who knows their VO₂max, their economy, their threshold and their EPOC can answer "why did that race fall apart in the last kilometre" with a mechanism rather than a guess. Coaches use them to set pace targets that are honest about what the athlete can actually deliver on the day, given their fitness, their fatigue and the weather. And they are all trainable, which is the hopeful part: every one of them responds to work.',
zh:'这四个数字，正是把比赛计划变成生理论证的东西。知道自己最大摄氧量、动作经济性、阈值和过量氧耗的运动员，能用机制而不是猜测来回答“为什么最后那一公里掉下来了”。教练用它们设定当天真正能兑现的配速目标——把体能、疲劳和天气都算进去。而这四项全部可训练，这也是最令人有希望的部分：每一项都会对训练作出回应。'}}
};
